\par\par \b \uc2 Hong Kong banks' usage of \uc2 HKMA\uc2  discount window hits 3-year high in July\b0\par\par\uc2 464 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2023\par 11:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Aug 3 (Reuters) - Hong Kong banks borrowed a record amount from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in July as demand from companies paying seasonal dividends and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the monetary authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets drove cash levels down.\par \par \uc2 The scramble for cash lifted interbank rates to a two decade-high and saw banks borrowing HK$8.2 billion ($1.05 billion) from the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's discount facility - the highest monthly amount in over three years.\par \par \uc2 The discount window is an overnight liquidity facility available to banks at the city's base rate, which is 5.75%, and currently higher than inter-bank rates.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said banks were forced to tap the discount window more than usual in July as listed corporates needed more \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars to pay dividends, even as the \uc2 HKMA\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg drove down the aggregate banking system balance.\par \par \uc2 The overnight Hong Kong Interbank Offered rate (HIHKDOND=) rose as far as 5.6%, a 17-year high in July, as cash conditions tightened.\par \par \uc2 "Banks normally wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t tap the discount window if the funding demand can be met in the interbank market," said Sam Wong, an analyst at Jefferies.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong\u8217\'20\'19s main policy rate, the base rate, is tethered to rates in the United States by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg that moves in a tight range of 7.75-7.85 per U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 As the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  raised interest rates in one of the fastest tightening cycles since the 1980s, Hong Kong's base rate has outpaced the rise in interbank rates, the latter more influenced by domestic demand and supply, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 But banks are also beginning to feel the pinch from a thinning aggregate balance, or interbank cash balance, which since May has stayed below HK$45 billion at the lowest levels since 2008.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 HKMA\uc2  does not disclose information about which banks have used the facilities, but banks with a higher ratio of liquid assets appear not to have needed to borrow from the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "With our liquidity coverage ratio at over 230% and above the regulatory requirement, we are able to plan our liquidity need for the second half, said Saw Say Pin, \uc2 Hang Seng Bank\uc2 's chief financial officer. "...we have not tapped the discount window".\par \par \uc2 The July spurt in use of the discount window may be a one-off, as the seasonal dividend pressures will fade after August and interbank rates would then likely ease, said Kiyong Seong, lead Asia macro strategist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . ($1 = 7.7982 Hong Kong dollars) (Reporting by Georgina Lee; Editing by Vidya Ranganathan & Simon Cameron-Moore) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-8-3T10:47:05.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230803ej8301ejm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
