\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Hong Kong interbank rates jump, pull HKD off band extreme\b0\par\par\uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2023\par 11:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds analysts' comment and Hong Kong dollar levels)\par \par \uc2 By Tom Westbrook\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, May 10 (Reuters) - Hong Kong interbank rates jumped on Wednesday as a further decline in banking system cash balances spurred expectations of tightening monetary settings and to an unwinding of short positions in the pegged Hong Kong dollar.\par \par \uc2 The overnight Hong Kong Interbank Offered Rate shot up nearly 92 basis points to 4.4381%, its highest since December 2019.\par \par \uc2 One-week HIBOR rose about 48 bps to 4.10952% and one-month HIBOR hit its highest since January at 4.15762%. The Hong Kong dollar rose to its strongest since the middle of February at 7.8325 per U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong rates are tethered to the U.S. by the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar, and have been rising for a few weeks. The city's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged to the greenback in a tight range of 7.75-7.85 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The spikes in interbank rates came on the back of the decline in the aggregate balance - a key gauge of cash balances in the banking system - which at HK$44.5 billion is now at its lowest level since 2008 and expected to drop further.\par \par \uc2 The aggregate balance has been falling steadily this year as the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the peg at 7.85 against the force of investors borrowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for carry trades.\par \par \uc2 Yet interbank rates were pinned to lows as weak loan demand and investment inflows into Chinese assets kept the banking system flush.\par \par \uc2 Analysts pointed to waning optimism on China and a decision by Hong Kong banks to raise their prime lending rates this month as reasons for the spike in Hibor.\par \par \uc2 Banks seemed to be selling down their more than a trillion Hong Kong dollars worth of holdings in the city's Exchange Fund Bills and Notes (EFBNs) to raise cash, driving yields on one-month bills up half a percentage point this month.\par \par \uc2 That in turn has caused 3-month spreads between U.S. dollar Libor and equivalent Hibor to compress to 130 basis points from a historically wide 175 points just a few weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 "The reversal of capital inflow to outflow (i.e. reducing HKD supply) from the reopening trade, if taking place, will pose upward pressure on HKD rates," wrote Ken Cheung, chief Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The narrowing of the U.S. and Hong Kong dollar rate spread prompted traders to unwind the carry trade positions."\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Georgina Lee in Hong Kong Editing by Vidya Ranganathan and Raju Gopalakrishnan) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-5-10T10:36:39.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230510ej5a02shv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
