\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority \highlight\uc2  Seems to Be Ramping Up  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,276 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Februar 2023\par 05:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0443 GMT - \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to be ramping up its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say. The \uc2 HKMA\uc2  bought HK$14.868 billion, the most since July 20, 2022, during the U.S. trading session overnight, says Ken Cheung, chief Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2 , in an email. Tuesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  follows \uc2 HKMA\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of HK$4.223 billion on Monday, Cheung notes. Tuesday's release of sticky U.S. inflation boosted Fed's rate increase expectations, sparking heavy selling of HKD in spot market, Cheung adds. USD/HKD is little changed at 7.8483. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0341 GMT - With a potential two more cash-rate hikes yet to come in Australia, \uc2 CBA\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Matt Comyn\uc2  says the country's homeowners have so far experienced only about half of the likely impact of higher rates on monthly cash flows. Speaking to investors after handing down the lender's 1H FY 2023 results, Comyn says \uc2 CBA\uc2  has seen some customers drawing on savings and reducing spending, but has yet to see this translate into customers falling behind on repayments. The lender reckons that the full impact of rapid interest-rate tightening has yet to emerge, and that there will likely be a moderation in consumer discretionary spending in the months ahead. "The overall credit environment remained very benign in the [1H] period, however we are watching closely for early signs of stress, particularly amongst high-risk cohorts," says Comyn.(alice.uribe@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0340 GMT - China's significant rise in household deposits has been driven by the government's fiscal and quasifiscal expansion, not by savings, say \uc2 China International Capital Corp\uc2 . analysts in a note addressing market concerns over excess household savings potentially impeding a recovery in consumption. \uc2 CICC\uc2  believes household deposits stemming from fiscal expansion will convert to investment and consumption, and remains optimistic about consumption in China. However, the analysts say the structure of excess deposits may lead to changes in consumption patterns and possibly the performance of the real-estate and stock markets. (bingyan.wang@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT - The yen strengthens against other G-10 and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid risk-off sentiment sparked by U.S. CPI data released overnight. The stronger-than-expected data have sent jitters to market participants, Tina Teng, markets analyst at \uc2 CMC Markets\uc2 , says in an email, noting that January headline inflation was 6.4% on year, higher than an estimated 6.2%. The resurgent inflation is certainly not good news for global markets, Teng adds. USD/JPY falls 0.3% to 132.71, AUD/JPY drops 0.6% to 92.47 and EUR/JPY loses 0.4% to 142.44. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0116 GMT - RBA Gov. \uc2 Philip Lowe\uc2  says the path to a soft landing for the economy remains open. In his testimony before the Senate, Lowe says inflation expectations remain anchored and wages growth stays relatively contained. \uc2 Lowe\uc2 's testimony comes amid calls for him to resign as \uc2 RBA\uc2  Gov. and media reporting about the risks to the economy due to higher interest rates. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0052 GMT - RBA Gov. \uc2 Philip Lowe\uc2  says he will serve out all of his seven-year term as head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In testimony before Australia's parliament, Lowe rejected the idea that he should resign because of earlier policy mistakes and an over-zealous drive to raise interest rates. \uc2 Lowe\uc2 's term ends in mid-September. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0051 GMT - Australian prime and nonconforming home-loan arrears rose in December and were more pronounced than in previous years, says S&P Global Ratings in a new report. "December is one of the peak months for higher arrears, with consumer spending elevated in the wake of Black Friday sales and the lead up to Christmas and the summer holiday period," it says. S&P reckons that arrears could remain elevated for longer because nonconforming borrowers will find it more difficult to self-manage their way out of arrears. "The arrears cycle typically peaks around January and February. The magnitude of arrears increases this year is likely to be greater than in recent years," it adds. (alice.uribe@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0049 GMT - \uc2 RBA\uc2  Gov. \uc2 Philip Lowe\uc2  has told the Australian Senate that monetary-policy settings are currently restrictive, but at the same time, that inflation is "way too high." \uc2 Lowe\uc2  says his intention isn't to drive the economy into recession by raising interest rates, adding that he hopes to retain recent gains in the job market. Still, he acknowledges that the \uc2 RBA\uc2  is walking a "narrow path." (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0038 GMT - South Korea's benchmark Kospi is down 0.5% to 2452.70 in early trade, dragged by internet and financial stocks. Growth stocks retreat after U.S. inflation eased a bit in January but remained higher than expected, which could warrant a prolonged period of the Fed's policy tightening. South Korea's slowing jobs growth also dampens sentiment. Foreign and institutional investors are net sellers. USD/KRW is 0.4% higher at 1,274.55. Internet platform giants \uc2 Naver\uc2  and \uc2 Kakao Corp\uc2 . fall 2.0% and 2.5%, respectively. \uc2 Hana Financial Group\uc2  sheds 3.3%. Meanwhile, electric-vehicle battery maker \uc2 LG Energy Solution\uc2  gains 2.1% after a rally in EV maker \uc2 Tesla\uc2  overnight.(kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0027 GMT - Japanese stocks are higher in early trade, led by gains in chip and chemical stocks, as a weaker yen raises hopes for earnings growth. Nippon Paint Holdings is up 9.8% after fourth-quarter net profit beat analysts' expectations. \uc2 Renesas Electronics\uc2  gains 5.6% and \uc2 Mitsubishi Chemical Group\uc2  is 4.0% higher. USD/JPY is at 132.84, up from 131.88 as of Tuesday's Tokyo stock market close. Meanwhile, \uc2 Shimano\uc2  is down 9.4% after it projects a 33% drop in net profit in 2023. Earnings are in focus, with \uc2 Sapporo Holdings\uc2  scheduled to announce its results later in the day. The Nikkei Stock Average is up 0.2% at 27649.53. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 2346 GMT - Japanese stocks may rise as a weaker yen raises hopes for earnings growth. Nikkei futures open up 95 points at 27725 on the SGX. Some financial stocks may outperform after the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields rose overnight on the back of elevated U.S. inflation data. USD/JPY is at 132.97, up from 131.88 as of Tuesday's Tokyo stock market close. Investors remain focused on quarterly results as the earnings season winds down. \uc2 Sapporo Holdings\uc2  is set to report its results later in the day. The Nikkei Stock Average rose 0.6% to 27602.77 on Tuesday. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT - Former \uc2 RBA\uc2  Governor Bernie Fraser has joined the growing chorus of those criticising the current \uc2 RBA\uc2  Governor \uc2 Philip Lowe\uc2  for misjudgment, saying in a radio interview that guidance issued by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week to expect a number of interest-rate increases over coming months may not have been the most prudent way to communicate. It's better to be cautious and prudent in the language to talk about future changes in interest rates or monetary policy, Fraser says. \uc2 Lowe\uc2  is facing calls to resign after raising interest rates at a record pace since May last year after earlier saying they might remain low until 2024. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 14, 2023 23:43 ET (04:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020230215ej2f0008r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
