\par\par \b \uc2 Bets Against Hong Kong's Dollar Peg Have Yet to Pay Off -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matthew Thomas \par \uc2 1,053 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Dezember 2022\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B9\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Bill Ackman\uc2  revealed last month that he was betting the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the greenback would break, he became the latest in a long line of speculators who have made similar wagers. Nobody has hit the jackpot.\par \par \uc2 Hedge funds have made bets against the longstanding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg going back to the Asian financial crisis. High-profile fund managers including \uc2 Crispin Odey\uc2  and \uc2 Kyle Bass\uc2  are among those who have previously taken positions that the peg couldn't last. Mr. Ackman's fund, \uc2 Pershing Square Capital Management\uc2 , even once made a bet from the opposite side more than a decade ago. \uc2 Boaz Weinstein\uc2 , the founder of Saba Capital Management LP, is betting alongside him this time.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar's value has been tied to the U.S. dollar since 1983 and it trades within a narrow range of 7.75 to 7.85 per dollar. That stable exchange rate has been a big draw for international investors to the Asian financial hub.\par \par \uc2 So far, the peg has stood firm and many economists and analysts from major global banks believe that will remain the case for the foreseeable future. They point to the determination of the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the peg in place -- and the track record of the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's $400-plus billion financial war chest, called the Exchange Fund, which has proved capable of protecting it in the past.\par \par \uc2 "At the moment, none of the data suggests the peg is under threat in any way," said Vikas Gupta, head of Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and emerging markets trading at \uc2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Proponents of the bet against the peg rely in part on a political argument: that China's government could become unhappy that one of its key financial centers has a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  linked to the U.S. -- a rival superpower. This was one of the points made by Mr. Ackman, who revealed his trade on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . He declined a request for further comment.\par \par \uc2 They also have an economic case. The peg has forced Hong Kong to relinquish control over its monetary policy and follow the interest-rate decisions of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . But at some point being tethered to the Fed could run counter to the needs of the local economy, warranting a break.\par \par \uc2 The Fed has raised interest rates by 4.25 percentage points this year in an effort to bring down high American inflation. Hong Kong, whose economy has contracted for three quarters in a row, has followed these interest rate increases in order to maintain the peg.\par \par \uc2 The peg is also costly to defend. The reserves of the HKMA's Exchange Fund have fallen by nearly $80 billion this year, partly because it has sold greenbacks to buy Hong Kong dollars whenever the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hits 7.85 to the dollar -- which happened numerous times in 2022.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 HKMA\uc2  has sold $31 billion in U.S. dollars so far this year, much more than the $13 billion it spent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 2018, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar weakened, according to data from the authority.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say those arguments ignore the positives of the peg and Hong Kong's ability to defend it. The $423 billion that the city had in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at the end of November is still more than enough to protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they add. The Exchange Fund's reserves are more than two-thirds larger than the size of Hong Kong's monetary base, a measure of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  circulating in the economy.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong has little to gain from pegging its dollar to China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, which is also shielded from external market forces. In mainland China, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows the yuan to trade within a daily range against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The offshore yuan is more freely traded, but it has much thinner liquidity and rarely moves far from the value of the onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yuan earlier this year weakened past 7.25 per dollar in October, before strengthening to 6.98 recently.\par \par \uc2 China is likely to be pragmatic about the role of the U.S. dollar peg in Hong Kong. Although Mr. Ackman's argument makes some sense, his timing is premature, said Ju Wang, head of Greater China \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rates strategy at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "When the yuan becomes fully floating and fully convertible, we may not need to have a Hong Kong dollar peg," Ms. Wang said. "But those conditions aren't fully met, so to have a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  linked to the dollar makes fundamental sense."\par \par \uc2 Rising interest rates at a time of economic contraction for Hong Kong are clearly a problem, but many economists think the Fed is nearing the end of its rate-raising cycle.\par \par \uc2 Barclays PLC\uc2  analysts Lemon Zhang and Ashish Argawal think the eventual fall in interbank rates in Hong Kong will lag behind those in the U.S., helping to support the Hong Kong dollar.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ackman has bet against the peg using put options, which give the holder the right to sell an asset at a specific price by a set time in the future. If the Hong Kong dollar is trading below that price when those options can be exercised, Mr. Ackman can lock in the difference between the price guaranteed by the option and the lower market price.\par \par \uc2 The use of options means hedge funds can get a large gain for a relatively small initial cost. Mr. Weinstein, of Saba Capital, said the trade is a "lottery ticket" offering returns of 200-to-1 or more.\par \par \uc2 Since the Hong Kong dollar is allowed to trade in a range against the dollar, these hedge funds could still make money without it leaving that range, although their returns would be much smaller than if the peg had broken.\par \par \uc2 Write to Matthew Thomas at matthew.thomas@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (December 20, 2022).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 20, 2022 02:32 ET (07:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020221220eick000gl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
