\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Billionaire investor Ackman bets Hong Kong dollar peg can break\b0\par\par\uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2022\par 07:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Pressure on Hong Kong dollar as U.S. rates rise\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong interest rates zoom as interbank liquidity dries up\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last loitering around the middle of the band\par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from Kyle Bass)\par \par \uc2 By Tom Westbrook\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Nov 24 (Reuters) - Billionaire investor \uc2 Bill Ackman\uc2  said he's betting the Hong Kong dollar will fall and that its peg to the U.S. dollar can break, the latest big money manager to take a public short bet as U.S. rate hikes turn the blowtorch on Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system.\par \par \uc2 "We have a large notional short position against the Hong Kong dollar through the ownership of put options," he said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . "The peg no longer makes sense for Hong Kong and it is only a matter of time before it breaks."\par \par \uc2 The details of Ackman's position were unclear. A spokesman for Ackman's fund, \uc2 Pershing Square\uc2 , declined to comment further.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar has been pegged in a tight band between 7.75 and 7.85 per greenback for nearly four decades and tends to face pressure - and thus far unsuccessful speculative challenges - every time U.S. interest rates go up.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintains the peg by moving interest rates in lockstep with the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  and by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which drains \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity and is designed to drive local rates up until inflows stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the month, Hong Kong's financial secretary sought to warn speculators.\par \par \uc2 "If you bet against the Hong Kong dollar, you are bound to lose," Paul Chan told an audience at an investment summit in the city.\par \par \uc2 However, some economists say the speed and scale of this hiking cycle is the sternest test yet, particularly as Chinese growth falters, making for an uncomfortable time to be raising rates.\par \par \uc2 U.S. fund manager Kyle Bass has long bet against the Hong Kong dollar and told Reuters on Thursday he had a fund dedicated to being short Hong Kong dollars versus the U.S.dollar.\par \par \uc2 "It's nice to finally have others agreeing with our thesis. Rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs harnessed to asynchronous economies are destined to fail," Bass said in an emailed response.\par \par \uc2 "One bad day of deposit/\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  outflows will likely bring unimaginable stress to the situation. The press and the \uc2 HKMA\uc2  like to tout 'Total Reserves' but just imagine if the \uc2 HKMA\uc2  had to sell HK stocks or private equity in order to defend the peg."\par \par \uc2 American investor George Soros is among those who have in the past bet against the peg, but his 1998 attack on the peg was in vain.\par \par \uc2 Liquidity is draining very fast as the \uc2 HKMA\uc2  has sucked up about $30 billion in some 40 rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the Fed began raising rates in March.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the aggregate balance - a key gauge of cash in the banking system - fell below HK$100 billion ($12.8 billion) for the first time since 2020.\par \par \uc2 The one-month Hong Kong Interbank Offer Rate currently stands at a 14-year high.\par \par \uc2 Since May, the Hong Kong dollar has been pinned near the weaker end of its band, although it has lifted a bit in recent weeks as markets start to price a peak in U.S. rates. It was last at 7.8142 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 A spokewoman at the \uc2 HKMA\uc2  said it would not comment on any individual's commentary about the peg.\par \par \uc2 However, "some market participants have at various times expressed their queries about HK's linked exchange rate system (LERS) over the years. These comments are primarily based on their own misunderstanding on the HK's system, or the position of their own books," she wrote in an email reply to Reuters.\par \par \uc2 The LERS has endured several economic cycles and periods of significant capital flows over its nearly 40 years of \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , continues to perform well and does not need to change, the \uc2 HKMA\uc2  said. ($1 = 7.8132 Hong Kong dollars)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tom Westbrook in Singapore and Georgina Lee in Hong Kong; Editing by Kim Coghill, Sam Holmes and Edmund Klamann) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-11-24T07:18:26.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221124eibo00cf9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
