\par\par \b \uc2 MORNING BID-Sky HIBOR\b0\par\par\uc2 401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2022\par 20:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nov 1 (Reuters) - A look at the day ahead in Asian markets from Jamie McGeever Investors' focus on the growing stress in Asian financial markets has tended to center gravitate towards the yuan and the yen. They should probably add HIBOR to their worries.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong Interbank Offered Rates - the rates at which banks in Hong Kong lend to each other - are soaring, especially at the short end of the curve. Indeed, the ultra short end is inverted.\par \par \uc2 Overnight HIBOR jumped by 58 basis points on Monday through 3.00%, the highest in almost three years. This followed a 45 bps spike on Friday.\par \par \uc2 What's going on?\par \par \uc2 At face value it suggests liquidity is drying up, pushing borrowing costs sharply higher. Rising credit risk could be related to Hong Kong banks' exposure to the crisis-hit Chinese property sector.\par \par \uc2 It could also be related to Hong Kong's efforts to defend its pegged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and prevent it from breaching the weaker end of its 7.75 to 7.85 band against the U.S. dollar. This has eroded cash balances in the local banking system to their lowest in nearly three years.\par \par \uc2 It could also be related to the mounting downward pressure on the Chinese yuan. Or month-end issues - HIBOR spiked sharply higher in late June and September too.\par \par \uc2 Whatever the cause, it is another regional red flag on top of persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the steep decline in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Chinese stocks, especially tech.\par \par \uc2 On the yen, \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed that it spent $43 billion on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over two days in October, which follows $20 billion spent on Sept. 22 trying to contain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide.\par \par \uc2 In sum: $63 billion spent, in three days, with the respective dollar/yen trigger points apparently around 146.00, 152.00, and 149.50. With dollar/yen currently around 148.65, it is an open question whether authorities' action has been a success.\par \par \uc2 The big regional diary event on Tuesday will be the \uc2 RBA\uc2 's rate decision. Investors are forecasting a quarter-point hike.\par \par \uc2 Three key developments that could provide more direction to markets on Tuesday:\par \par \uc2 Regional PMIs (October)\par \par \uc2 Australia rate decision (25 bps hike expected)\par \par \uc2 U.S. ISM (October)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Jamie McGeever in Orlando, Fla.; Editing by Josie Kao) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-31T20:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221031eiav041lq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
