\par\par \b \uc2 HCL Technologies\uc2  First-Quarter Net Profit Rose 2.4%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ronnie Harui \par \uc2 904 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2022\par 02:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 HCL Technologies Ltd\uc2 .'s net profit rose 2.4% in its fiscal first quarter, mainly owing to robust revenue growth at the IT and business services segment and the engineering and research and development services segment.\par \par \uc2 The Indian IT services and consulting company said late Tuesday that net profit was 32.83 billion rupees ($413.1 million) for the three months ended June 30, up from net profit of INR32.05 billion for the same period a year earlier. Total income rose to INR238.73 billion from INR203.23 billion a year ago.\par \par \uc2 HCL Technologies\uc2  said it expects fiscal 2023 revenue to grow between 12% and 14% in constant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  terms and its Ebit margin to be between 18% and 20%.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ronnie Harui at ronnie.harui@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 14 Jul 2022 03:54 ET  Asian Central Banks Unveil Surprise Tightening to Fight Inflation\par \par \uc2 By Ronnie Harui\par \par \uc2 A pair of Asian central banks made surprise moves to tighten policy to rein in fast-rising inflation, while another \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's interest-rate increases boost the appeal of the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 In near back-to-back moves Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore\uc2  tightened its policy while the \uc2 Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its benchmark rates by 75 basis points, both citing the need to curb fast-rising inflation.\par \par \uc2 Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  opted to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen by recentering upward the midpoint of the policy band for the Singapore dollar nominal effective exchange rate. It said overall inflationary pressure will remain elevated in the months ahead, and raised its forecasts for both core and headline inflation in the Southeast Asian city-state for this year.\par \par \uc2 Unlike most central banks, the MAS uses the exchange rate instead of interest rates as it is a more effective policy tool for maintaining price stability owing to Singapore's small and open economy.\par \par \uc2 "There remain upside risks to inflation from fresh shocks to global commodity prices and domestic wage pressures," the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement. The policy tightening "should help slow the momentum of inflation and ensure medium-term price stability," the MAS added.\par \par \uc2 The off-cycle move marked the MAS's fourth straight round of tightening after it tightened policy at regular meetings in October and April and at another unscheduled meeting in January amid rising inflationary pressures in the regional financial hub.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar, which like many Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been weakened by rising rates in the U.S., rose as much as 0.8% against the U.S. dollar in the wake of the news.\par \par \uc2 "MAS caught markets by surprise," Jeff Ng, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2 , said in a research report. The decision was "a more significant move than what we had anticipated," he said, adding that he expects further moves by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its next scheduled meeting in October. The MAS usually holds meetings twice a year, in April and in October.\par \par \uc2 Several hours after the MAS decision, the \uc2 Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly raised its interest rates, saying significant tightening was needed amid signs of sustained, broadening price pressures.\par \par \uc2 The country's consumer-price index in June rose 6.1% from a year earlier, well above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation target range of 2%-4%. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in May raised its policy rate for the first time in more than three years, by 25 basis points, and followed with another 25-basis-point increase in June.\par \par \uc2 The move by \uc2 Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas\uc2  "might be to address concerns that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was behind the curve in addressing inflation concerns, especially with rising bets for outsized Fed hikes near-term," Maybank analysts said in a research report.\par \par \uc2 Both moves came after news that the U.S. consumer-price index rose 9.1% in June from a year earlier, marking the fastest pace in more than four decades and raising market expectations for an aggressive rate hike at the Fed's next meeting later this month.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it sold U.S. dollars during New York hours on Wednesday to defend the financial hub's longstanding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the greenback.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 HKMA\uc2 , which is the city's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said it sold US$1.63 billion against the Hong Kong dollar at the 7.8500 level. The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has now sold HK$117.075 billion (US$14.91 billion) worth of U.S. dollars since its first \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of this year on May 11, official data showed.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been tied to the U.S. dollar since 1983 and trades within a permitted range of 7.75 to 7.85 Hong Kong dollars per U.S. dollar. The \uc2 HKMA\uc2  sells U.S. dollars if the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gets too weak or buys them if the Hong Kong dollar gets too strong.\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the Hong Kong dollar, have been under heavy pressure as the Fed raises interest rates more aggressively than other central banks in order to contain decades-high inflation, boosting the allure of U.S. dollar-denominated fixed-income assets.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ronnie Harui at ronnie.harui@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 14, 2022 03:54 ET (07:54 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220713ei7d0005p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw109\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1635\pichgoal600
