\par\par \b \uc2 Hong Kong shares slide, China stocks and yuan find footing\b0\par\par\uc2 726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2022\par 10:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  comment, update prices to close)\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, May 10 (Reuters) - Hong Kong shares tumbled on Tuesday following a one-day holiday, playing catch-up with falls on Wall Street, while China stocks rebounded on Beijing's vows to support the struggling economy, with signs of bargain hunting seen in both markets.\par \par \uc2 The yuan stabilised after earlier slipping to a new 18-month low, as China's benchmark money market rates flirted with 16-month lows.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's benchmark \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  Index fell as much as 4.1%, before recovering some losses to end trading down 1.8%. The Hang Seng Tech Index slumped 3.2%.\par \par \uc2 Both China's blue chip CSI300 Index and the Shanghai Composite Index ended 1.1% higher.\par \par \uc2 "All eyes are on U.S. stocks," said Linus Yip, chief strategist at First Shanghai Group.\par \par \uc2 "If Wall Street keeps falling, it would definitely be a drag on global markets, including Hong Kong and China."\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 Index has dropped more than 3% this week amid fears of stagflation and rising U.S. interest rates.\par \par \uc2 However, Yip points to signs that mainland money has been flowing into Hong Kong stocks over the past week due to bargain-hunting.\par \par \uc2 "Hong Kong shares have corrected much earlier than U.S. stocks, so their valuation is much lower," he said.\par \par \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  trades at less than 10 times earnings, compared with more than 20 for the S&P 500.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the Hong Kong dollar briefly touched 7.85 per dollar, the weak end of its trading band on Tuesday, before edging back without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the local de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar is pegged to the greenback, and its weakness signifies capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 UTMOST PESSIMISM\par \par \uc2 China stocks rebounded after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  vowed on Monday further support for the slowing economy, and measures to boost confidence.\par \par \uc2 Li Bei, hedge fund manager at Shanghai Banxia Investment Management, said that following forced selling, liquidation, and loss-cutting by local investors, China's bear market has entered its final stage.\par \par \uc2 "The bloody sell-off has knocked stock valuations to historic lows that priced in the worst economic outlook, and reflected utmost pessimism," she wrote.\par \par \uc2 "The downward momentum has been nearly exhausted. Any positive element could trigger a robust rebound."\par \par \uc2 China's tech-heavy STAR50 index rebounded 2.7%, led by chipmakers, while start-up board ChiNext gained 2.2%.\par \par \uc2 Yang Hongxun, analyst at investment consultancy Shandong Shenguang, attributed the rebound to expectations of policy support for the sector following news Washington will step up sanctions against Chinese semiconductor companies.\par \par \uc2 "This will result in more government support to home-grown chipmakers," he said.\par \par \uc2 Many foreign investors, however, remain circumspect on China with domestic investors also rebalancing their portfolios.\par \par \uc2 Latest fundraising data shows Chinese investors are shunning equity funds and piling into bonds, deposits and money market products.\par \par \uc2 BLACKROCK DOWNGRADE\par \par \uc2 Global fund giant \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  said it had reduced its overall portfolio level risk-taking, partly due to a worsening growth outlook in China.\par \par \uc2 "We're also downgrading our views on China assets including China government bonds from modestly overweight to neutral, as we view this asset class as being more risky," \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 's global chief investment strategist Wei Li said in a video commentary posted on its website.\par \par \uc2 The yuan strengthened against the dollar despite the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  setting the daily midpoint rate at 6.7134 per dollar, the weakest since Oct. 30, 2020.\par \par \uc2 "The path of least resistance could remain to the downside in the near-term as China sticks to targeted policy supports and there is little moderation of restrictive COVID measures," analysts at Maybank said.\par \par \uc2 "That being said, just as zero-COVID strategy is likely to be moderated only very gradually, officials may also prefer to step up on more growth supports from here on and that could keep a check on aggressive CNY declines."\par \par \uc2 China's seven-day repo rate, the benchmark money market rate, stood at 1.5479% on a weighted basis, flirting with the lowest level since late 2020, as the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has pledged to keep liquidity reasonably ample and prioritise stability. (Reporting by Shanghai Newsroom; Editing by Jacqueline Wong) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-5-10T09:22:07.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220510ei5a01c2n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
