\par\par \b \uc2 HKD May Fall to 7.85 Against USD as Soon as Early May, \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2  Says -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2022\par 07:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0516 GMT - The HKD may fall to 7.85 against the USD as soon as early May after the FOMC meeting, says \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2  in a research report, noting that the USD-HKD interest-rate spread was the key driver of the HKD's weakness during the Fed's previous rate-increase cycle. With an aggressive Fed rate-increase plan expected by mid-June, this will likely soon bring back the carry trade of long USD/HKD. The Fed's simultaneous balance-sheet reduction at a fast pace will also probably squeeze USD liquidity. Under \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Linked Exchange Rate System, \uc2 HKMA\uc2  will passively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the HKD to defend the USD/HKD peg at 7.85, the weak-side convertibility undertaking, the Japanese bank adds. USD/HKD is steady at 7.8446 after touching 7.8457 this week, its highest intraday level since October 2019, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT - JPY weakens against most G-10 and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid a mild risk-on mood spurred by gains in U.S. stock futures. Focus is likely to be on the upcoming meeting between Japanese Finance Minister Suzuki and \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Secretary Yellen. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  coordination will be on the agenda, SPI Asset Management managing partner Stephen Innes says in an e-mail. However, he is skeptical that anything like a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will occur, "as the U.S. doesn't necessarily mind a strong dollar at this point, not to mention the JPY is weak due to \uc2 BOJ\uc2  policy." USD/JPY rises 0.4% to 128.40, SGD/JPY gains 0.3% to 94.11 and AUD/JPY is up 0.2% at 95.48. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2335 GMT - New Zealand's 1Q inflation rate of 6.9% supports expectations for a series of \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  interest rate increases over the coming months, says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  economist \uc2 Satish Ranchhod\uc2  . The outcome was a little softer than the market consensus, but higher than \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  's forecast from February. "Price pressures are bubbling over in every corner of the economy," says Ranchhod. It means the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  --which raised its cash rate by 50 bps to 1.5% last week--will follow up with another 50 bp increase in May and 25 bp increases at subsequent meetings, he says. (stephen.wright@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2047 GMT - The IPC stock index falls 1.5% to 53831 points, its lowest close in a month. Bellwether America Movil shares close off 3.1%, and miner Grupo Mexico shares end off 4.8%. \uc2 Cemex\uc2  shares advance 1.7%. The peso makes modest gains, trading at 19.99 against the US dollar versus 20.03 yesterday. Banks polled by Citibanamex cut their 2022 GDP growth forecast to 1.8% from 1.9% previously, and raised the median year-end inflation estimate to 6.38% from 5.98%. All expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue raising interest rates, with 32 of the 34 predicting another 50bp increase to 7% in May. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1853 GMT - The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  flagged a slightly alarming detail in the release of its latest Beige Book report Wednesday. As part of its commentary on the inflation, the report noted "a few reports noted that input suppliers were making use of more flexible contract terms or only honoring price quotes for 24 hours." That suggests at least some firms felt they have enough pricing power to actively tweak prices, or are facing fast moving factors that require constant changes in what they charge. Either way, that's not a great fit for the Fed's price stability goals. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1831 GMT - The value of freight shipped across the U.S. borders with Canada and Mexico in February was valued at $112.5B, the US Bureau of Transportation says. That figure was 17.3% higher than the year-ago period but down 1.1% from the prior month. The value of the freight was up 17.2% from pre-pandemic Feb. 2020, according to BTS. Trucks moved $69.2 billion of freight, up 16.3% from Feb. 2021. The top three commodities moved by truck were computers and parts, electrical machinery and vehicles and parts, the BTS said. Rail freight totaled $15.3 billion, up 19% from the year-ago period, the BTS said, adding the top three commodities carried by rail were vehicles and parts, mineral fuels, and plastics. The data used by the BTS is not seasonally adjusted and not adjusted for inflation. (stephen.nakrosis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1801 GMT - Americans are buying smaller homes than they were at the height of the pandemic, \uc2 Redfin\uc2  says. The typical US home that went under contract in March was 1,720 square feet, down 1.8% from 1,751 square feet the same time last year and roughly the same size as the homes that were selling just before the pandemic began. Smaller homes are typically cheaper than larger homes, and that puts limitations on buyers and what they want in a home. "If buyers don't want to compromise on location, they probably need to settle for a smaller home," says \uc2 Redfin\uc2  . The median price per square foot increased 20.1% to a record $230 in March. Homebuyers are also turning back to condos, one factor in the shrinking square footage of homes that are selling. (chris.wack@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1745 GMT - The median monthly asking rent in the US increased 17% year-over-year to a record high of $1,940 in March, \uc2 Redfin\uc2  says, the largest annual jump since at least February 2020. Meanwhile, the median monthly mortgage payment for homebuyers rose twice as fast, climbing 34% year-over-year to $1,910. Mortgage rates climbed from under 3% to 5% in three months, pushing many potential buyers out of the for-sale market and increasing demand and prices for rentals. "Many potential first-time homebuyers are quickly being priced out of the market by record-high home prices and fast-increasing mortgage rates," \uc2 Redfin\uc2  says. Options then become rent or move somewhere with a lower cost of living. Renters may save money in the near term, but long term, they may have to deal with continued rent increases, year after year, says \uc2 Redfin\uc2  . (chris.wack@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1726 GMT - North American freight railroad traffic slides 7.5% for the week ended Saturday, marking an eighth straight weekly decline. Carloads fall 6.8% on 12 reporting US, Canadian and Mexican railroads, while the volume of intermodal units drops 8.1%, data from the \uc2 Association of American Railroads\uc2  trade group shows. Analysts Jonathan Chappell and Sean Morgan at Evercore ISI note that tough comps and drought-impacted Canadian harvests weigh heavily on food and grain shipments, while service issues continue to pressure intermodal business. Coal and motor vehicles are the only freight categories to eke out gains for the week. North American rail traffic is down 3.9% for the first 15 weeks of the year, the \uc2 AAR\uc2  says. (colin.kellaher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1539 GMT - Home price growth in the UK is expected to slow, \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  says in a report. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  said it was expecting home price growth to slow to 1%-3% a year, down from 10.8% in 2021. While supportive mortgage conditions have helped drive UK home price growth over the past decade, this is set to change, according to \uc2 Fitch\uc2  . "Rising mortgage interest rates combined with limited scope to materially extend repayment term or loan to value are likely to weaken mortgage affordability for borrowers seeking new loans," \uc2 Fitch\uc2  said. In addition, households are facing inflationary pressures, driven in part by rising energy prices, according to \uc2 Fitch\uc2  . (stephen.nakrosis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1520 GMT - Brazil's employed population has risen to about where it was before the start of the pandemic as social distancing and other restrictions have eased, and further employment growth will depend on the pace of GDP growth, economists at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  said in a note. Even as employment has returned to pre-pandemic levels, the size of the labor force hasn't, because of concern about Covid-19 levels and high levels of household savings, according to the bank. That has left the jobless rate lower than it was at the start of the pandemic, but those factors are likely to have less effect going forward, so the size of the labor force should begin to recover in coming months, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  said. Brazil's unemployment rate was 11.2% in February, compared with 11.8% in February 2020, just before the start of the pandemic. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1430 GMT - The Australian dollar and New Zealand dollar are among the best-performing G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar so far this year but are likely to eventually weaken, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says. The AUD and NZD should hold their ground against the USD this year but depreciate thereafter as commodity prices fall and investors gradually pare back expectations for monetary policy tightening in Australia and New Zealand, Capital Economics economist James Reilly says in a research note. "Our end-2022, end-2023, and end-2024 forecasts for AUD/USD are 0.74, 0.72 and 0.70, respectively; and for NZD/USD, they are 0.68, 0.65, and 0.62, respectively." AUD/USD rises 0.7% to 0.7427 and NZD/USD rises 0.9% to 0.6791.(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 21, 2022 01:16 ET (05:16 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220421ei4l0009a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
