\par\par \b \uc2 FTSE 100 Poised to Edge Higher After Mixed Asia Trading -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2022\par 08:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0638 GMT - The FTSE 100 looks set to open slightly higher at 7637, according to IG futures data, after mixed trading in Asia and a higher close on Wall Street. Markets in mainland China and Hong Kong fall more than 2%, though Japan's Nikkei is up 1.2%. Brent crude gains 1.2% to $108.12 a barrel. The Dow closed 0.7% higher Wednesday. "Thursday's European open looks set to be a mixed one as we look ahead to comments from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holy trinity of Fed chair \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  , \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  and \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  governor Andrew Bailey, who are all due to speak in Washington DC," \uc2 CMC Markets\uc2  analyst Michael Hewson says. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0629 GMT - S&P Global Ratings is likely to upgrade Greece either on Friday or at the next review on Oct. 21, \uc2 Citi\uc2  's rates strategists say in a note. "This view is informed by recent improvements in the macroeconomic outlook for Greece, with \uc2 IMF\uc2  loans having been repaid, stable political backdrop and signs of a strong recovery in tourism revenues this year," the strategists say. S&P currently rates Greece at 'BB' with a positive outlook. "An upgrade could be particularly supportive for Greek government bonds, with only one more upgrade needed to reach IG [investment grade]," they say. \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  , which upgraded the outlook on Greece's 'BB' rating to positive in January, could also follow suit, \uc2 Citi\uc2  's strategists add. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0624 GMT - French government bonds on offer at Thursday's auction show concession on the domestic curve, \uc2 Citi\uc2  's rates strategists say in a note. Cash flowing back to investors could also provide support to demand, they add, pointing to EUR10.5 billion of coupon payments and EUR42.4 billion of redemptions that are eligible for reinvestment. The French treasury agency will auction EUR10 billion-EUR11 billion in February 2025-, February 2027- and May 2030-dated OATs. In a separate auction, it will also offer EUR1 billion-EUR1.5 billion in July 2031-, March 2036- and July 2053-dated inflation-linked bonds. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0619 GMT - Japan's Nikkei Stock Average ended 1.2% higher at 27553.06, led by gains in electronics and machinery stocks amid easing concerns over higher borrowing costs. Nikkei has been helped by the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  conducting rate-capping \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the JGB market, said Oanda's senior market analyst \uc2 Jeffrey Halley\uc2  in an email. \uc2 Lasertec Corp\uc2  . climbed 6.3%, \uc2 Disco Corp\uc2  . rose 4.3% and Daifuku gained 4.1%, while \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  added 3.6% and \uc2 Renesas Electronics\uc2  rose 3.05%. USD/JPY was at 128.17, compared with 128.69 as of Wednesday's Tokyo stock market close. The 10-year Japanese government bond yield was unchanged at 0.250%. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0553 GMT - USD/SGD is trading steadily in the afternoon Asian session, but could be weighed by prospects of peaking U.S. inflation. There's speculation that inflation in the U.S. may have peaked, and markets appear to have priced in an aggressive pace of Fed tightening, says Maybank in a research report. On the daily technical chart, USD/SGD's momentum is mildly bullish although the relative strength index isn't showing a clear bias, adds Maybank, which pegs initial support at 1.3625 and initial resistance at 1.3690. USD/SGD is little changed at 1.3626. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0538 GMT - Pandemic challenges may prompt the Chinese government to review its 5.5% GDP growth target and consider a more flexible goal, \uc2 Fitch Solutions\uc2  says in a note. While Beijing may continue to provide strong fiscal and monetary policy support, the research group no longer thinks that will be enough to propel growth above 5.0% in 2022. "The spreading of pandemic-related lockdowns will weigh heavily on economic activities over the coming quarters, exacerbating the impact of other headwinds including higher commodity prices and rising financial risks in the real estate sector." \uc2 Fitch\uc2  lowers its 2022 GDP growth forecast for China to 4.5% from 5.2% and now expects private consumption growth at 4.5%, compared with 6.0% forecast previously. (farah.elias@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0516 GMT - The HKD may fall to 7.85 against the USD as soon as early May after the FOMC meeting, says \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2  in a research report, noting that the USD-HKD interest-rate spread was the key driver of the HKD's weakness during the Fed's previous rate-increase cycle. With an aggressive Fed rate-increase plan expected by mid-June, this will likely soon bring back the carry trade of long USD/HKD. The Fed's simultaneous balance-sheet reduction at a fast pace will also probably squeeze USD liquidity. Under \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Linked Exchange Rate System, \uc2 HKMA\uc2  will passively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the HKD to defend the USD/HKD peg at 7.85, the weak-side convertibility undertaking, the Japanese bank adds. USD/HKD is steady at 7.8446 after touching 7.8457 this week, its highest intraday level since October 2019, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT - JPY weakens against most G-10 and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid a mild risk-on mood spurred by gains in U.S. stock futures. Focus is likely to be on the upcoming meeting between Japanese Finance Minister Suzuki and \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Secretary Yellen. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  coordination will be on the agenda, SPI Asset Management managing partner Stephen Innes says in an e-mail. However, he is skeptical that anything like a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will occur, "as the U.S. doesn't necessarily mind a strong dollar at this point, not to mention the JPY is weak due to \uc2 BOJ\uc2  policy." USD/JPY rises 0.4% to 128.40, SGD/JPY gains 0.3% to 94.11 and AUD/JPY is up 0.2% at 95.48. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2335 GMT - New Zealand's 1Q inflation rate of 6.9% supports expectations for a series of \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  interest rate increases over the coming months, says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  economist \uc2 Satish Ranchhod\uc2  . The outcome was a little softer than the market consensus, but higher than \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  's forecast from February. "Price pressures are bubbling over in every corner of the economy," says Ranchhod. It means the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  --which raised its cash rate by 50 bps to 1.5% last week--will follow up with another 50 bp increase in May and 25 bp increases at subsequent meetings, he says. (stephen.wright@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2047 GMT - The IPC stock index falls 1.5% to 53831 points, its lowest close in a month. Bellwether America Movil shares close off 3.1%, and miner Grupo Mexico shares end off 4.8%. \uc2 Cemex\uc2  shares advance 1.7%. The peso makes modest gains, trading at 19.99 against the US dollar versus 20.03 yesterday. Banks polled by Citibanamex cut their 2022 GDP growth forecast to 1.8% from 1.9% previously, and raised the median year-end inflation estimate to 6.38% from 5.98%. All expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue raising interest rates, with 32 of the 34 predicting another 50bp increase to 7% in May. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1853 GMT - The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  flagged a slightly alarming detail in the release of its latest Beige Book report Wednesday. As part of its commentary on the inflation, the report noted "a few reports noted that input suppliers were making use of more flexible contract terms or only honoring price quotes for 24 hours." That suggests at least some firms felt they have enough pricing power to actively tweak prices, or are facing fast moving factors that require constant changes in what they charge. Either way, that's not a great fit for the Fed's price stability goals. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1831 GMT - The value of freight shipped across the U.S. borders with Canada and Mexico in February was valued at $112.5B, the US Bureau of Transportation says. That figure was 17.3% higher than the year-ago period but down 1.1% from the prior month. The value of the freight was up 17.2% from pre-pandemic Feb. 2020, according to BTS. Trucks moved $69.2 billion of freight, up 16.3% from Feb. 2021. The top three commodities moved by truck were computers and parts, electrical machinery and vehicles and parts, the BTS said. Rail freight totaled $15.3 billion, up 19% from the year-ago period, the BTS said, adding the top three commodities carried by rail were vehicles and parts, mineral fuels, and plastics. The data used by the BTS is not seasonally adjusted and not adjusted for inflation. (stephen.nakrosis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 21, 2022 02:38 ET (06:38 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220421ei4l000ht\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
