ar  \par Credit-Risk Return Doesn't Have to Be Unwanted Sequel \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Delinquencies and charge-offs of some consumer loans will likely rise in 2022, but banks are in a position to handle a steady increase. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Gold Heads Toward Largest Percentage Decline Since 2015 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors expect rising interest rates and a climbing U.S. dollar to pressure the metal. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Some Companies Are Ready for Libor's Demise, but Not All \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. banks won't be allowed to issue new debt tied to the benchmark in 2022, leaving some firms struggling to decide how to proceed. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Big Tech's $10 Trillion Bet on Politics as Usual \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Soaring market values reflect growing belief that regulations won't trip \f28 \uc2 \f2 Apple\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Facebook\f28 \uc2 \f2  or \f28 \uc2 \f2 Amazon\f28 \uc2 \f2  .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 31, 2021 03:00 ET (08:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020211231ehcv000us\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
