\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4- \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  highest since 2009\b0\par\par\uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2020\par 06:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds analyst quote)\par \par \uc2 By Alun John\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Oct 14 (Reuters) - Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold more of its local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year than it did in any full year since the global financial crisis, in a bid to stop the unit strengthening and breaking its peg with the greenback.\par \par \uc2 Capital has rushed into the Asian financial hub in 2020, drawn in by comparatively high interest rates and a series of large initial public offerings, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Upcoming IPOs including Ant Group's looming $35 billion joint listing in Hong Kong and Shanghai are expected to keep demand for Hong Kong dollars high.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) sold HK$10.9 billion ($1.41 billion) on Tuesday in \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  during \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  and U.S. trading hours.\par \par \uc2 That brings the total sold so far this year to HK$230.6 billion, beating the HK$227 billion for the full year of 2015, which was previously the highest since 2009, according to official data and Reuters calculations.\par \par \uc2 This is a sharp turnaround from 2019, when some feared political uncertainty would drive money out of Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar is pegged in a narrow range of 7.75-7.85 to the U.S. dollar. When it weakens to HK$7.85 a dollar, the HKMA buys Hong Kong dollars from the market, as it did in 2018 and 2019.\par \par \uc2 When it strengthens to HK$7.75, the HKMA sells Hong Kong dollars.\par \par \uc2 Analysts warn those inflows could reverse if the United States issued further sanctions on financial institutions or individuals based in the territory due to Washington's concerns about the curtailment of political freedoms in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "Trump has been coming under heavier pressure to regain momentum in his re-election bid and is tempted to impose sanctions on Hong Kong," said Ken Cheung, chief Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2 . "Any move to undermine market confidence in the USD-HKD peg or HK's international financial centre status will disrupt capital flows."\par \par \uc2 Carie Li, economist at \uc2 OCBC Wing Hang Bank\uc2  in Hong Kong, said the factors driving the inflows had varied throughout 2020.\par \par \uc2 "Back in April it was about the 'carry trade' as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  cut rates to nearly zero, but Hong Kong interest rates did not follow the trend immediately," she said.\par \par \uc2 This meant traders sold U.S. dollars for Hong Kong dollars to benefit from the better rates.\par \par \uc2 "At the end of June, there were strong equity inflows...and then a very strong IPO pipeline which resulted in very strong HK dollar demand," Li said.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 7.7500 Hong Kong dollars)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Alun John; Editing by Sam Holmes) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-10-14T05:43:23.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020201014egae0012x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
