\par\par \b \uc2 Tech Firms Lift Hong Kong Bid For New Listings -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joanne Chiu \par \uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2020\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B11\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (June 12, 2020).\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's status as a venue for major Chinese technology companies got a boost Thursday, as shares in mobile-games group \uc2 NetEase Inc\uc2 . jumped on their debut in the city, while e-commerce company \uc2 JD.com Inc\uc2 . fixed a price for its own US$3.9 billion offering.\par \par \uc2 The duo are following \uc2 Alibaba Group Holding Ltd\uc2 . in securing secondary listings close to their home market, and the deals come as tensions between the U.S. and China widen to include financial-markets issues. The Senate in May passed a bill that could kick Chinese companies off U.S. stock exchanges unless American authorities can inspect their audits.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the listings are proceeding as the prospect of a Beijing-imposed national-security law raises questions about the city's future as an international financial and commercial hub. Robust demand for the new shares has buoyed the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, prompting official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the pegged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthening too much.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, shares in \uc2 NetEase Inc\uc2 . closed 5.7% higher, after its Hong Kong stock traded for the first time following a US$2.7 billion fundraising. Meanwhile, people familiar with the matter said \uc2 JD.com\uc2  has guided investors it plans to price its stock sale at 226 Hong Kong dollars (US$29.15) a share -- implying a total value of about US$3.9 billion, ahead of a trading debut in a week's time.\par \par \uc2 The offerings are a boost for exchange-operator \uc2 Hong Kong Exchanges and Clearing Ltd\uc2 ., which in 2018 revamped its rules to allow founder-friendly corporate-governance structures, such as shares with unequal voting rights.\par \par \uc2 With Alibaba, JD, \uc2 Meituan Dianping\uc2 , NetEase and \uc2 Tencent Holdings Ltd\uc2 ., the Hong Kong market will be home to five Chinese tech firms with market valuations of more than US$50 billion each.\par \par \uc2 "While a U.S. listing enables issuers to build a profile with international investors, a potential second listing in Hong Kong can help to further broaden their shareholder base, such as to include day-to-day customers of their apps and services," said Johnson Chui, head of equity capital markets for Asia Pacific at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bank is one of three sponsors for the NetEase deal. Mr. Chui said listing-rule changes and moves to allow companies with super-voting rights into the benchmark Hang Seng Index have increased the appeal of a secondary listing in Hong Kong for U.S.-listed Chinese companies.\par \par \uc2 A Hong Kong listing also means investors can trade nearly 24 hours a day, given the different trading hours for U.S.-listed American depositary receipts and for Hong Kong-listed shares, he said.\par \par \uc2 Depositary receipts in both \uc2 JD.com\uc2  and NetEase have soared this year, giving the companies market valuations of about US$86 billion and US$55 billion, respectively. For both deals in Hong Kong, bankers have the option to increase the eventual deal size by 15%.\par \par \uc2 JD.com\uc2 's shares will carry the ticker 9618.HK, and as is customary for follow-on share offerings, they were priced slightly cheaper than the shares outstanding. The offer price represents a 3.9% discount to the price implied by Wednesday's closing price for \uc2 JD.com\uc2 's American depositary receipts. Each ADR is equivalent to two ordinary shares in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 The share sales have helped boost the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So far in June the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold HK$27.4 billion for U.S. dollars, keeping the city's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening beyond 7.75 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 In a note to clients dated Thursday, analysts at China Renaissance said other large, U.S.-listed Chinese firms eligible to list in Hong Kong included \uc2 Baidu Inc\uc2 ., \uc2 TAL Education Group\uc2 , \uc2 ZTO Express\uc2  (Cayman) Inc., \uc2 New Oriental Education & Technology Group Inc\uc2 ., and \uc2 Trip.com Group Ltd\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Write to Joanne Chiu at joanne.chiu@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 12, 2020 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200612eg6c000gd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
