\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong \highlight\uc2  Dollar's Surge Spurs HKMA  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ronnie Harui \par \uc2 223 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 2020\par 04:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the U.S. dollar, selling the Hong Kong dollar after it hit the strong end of the trading band against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 The city's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold US$472 million of Hong Kong dollars at 7.75 a U.S. dollar during New York trading hours, injecting liquidity into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Including earlier \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  of HK$4.26 billion (US$549.7 million) and HK$1.43 billion, the latest action will increase the aggregate balance of the funds parked by banks in clearing accounts at the HKMA by HK$9.35 billion to HK$113.82 billion as of June 10.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar's appreciation is likely driven by funding demand for initial public offering subscriptions. The U.S. dollar was little changed at HK$7.7502 in early Asian trading Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg allows the Hong Kong dollar to trade between 7.75 and 7.85 against the U.S. dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys or sells the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  whenever it touches either side of the band.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ronnie Harui at ronnie.harui@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 09-06-20 0244GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020200609