\par\par \b \uc2 Hong Kong Tensions Are Escalating After a Protester Was Shot. Investors Should Brace for More Violence. -- Barrons.com\b0\par\par\uc2 By Reshma Kapadia \par \uc2 790 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2019\par 18:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Clashes between pro-democracy protesters and police in Hong Kong turned violent overnight with intense fighting on the streets and Hong Kong police shooting a protester. That leaves investors grappling with what could come next.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 iShares MSCI Hong Kong ETF\uc2  (EWH) fell 1.4% in recent trading to $22.38, while the \uc2 iShares MSCI China ETF\uc2  (MCHI) was down 0.66% at $55.71.\par \par \uc2 Demonstrations entered their 17th week, drawing tens of thousands overnight in counterrallies to the mass celebrations by China for the 70th anniversary of Communist Party rule where President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  offered a vision of strength and a unified country.\par \par \uc2 The mass protests began in June after the introduction of a bill that would allow the extradition of Hong Kong residents to the mainland. The bill was eventually withdrawn, but the demonstrations have continued, pushing back on what is seen as China's tightening grip over Hong Kong and chipping away at its autonomy under the "one country, two systems" created in the British handover of Hong Kong in 1997.\par \par \uc2 The protests have crippled Hong Kong's economy, which was already hurting from China's slowdown, and have left investors trying to gauge whether the situation could devolve into another version of the Tiananmen Square massacre and jeopardize Hong Kong's status as a global financial center.\par \par \uc2 "The shooting is a clear escalation and adds a major hurdle for authorities attempting to defuse the protests," says Rory Green, a TS Lombard economist. He expects calls to intensify for an independent investigation into alleged police misconduct, one of five demands laid out by protesters. But Hong Kong Chief Executive \uc2 Carrie Lam\uc2  "is not able to accede to this demand without appearing to abandon the police," Green says. "The contradiction will serve to worsen relations between protesters and the government."\par \par \uc2 Escalation and further mass protests are likely, but Green doesn't think China will bring in the People's Liberation Army to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as continued protests don't pose an economic or political threat to mainland China.\par \par \uc2 The same can't be said if Beijing does \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Such a move could cause a financial shock in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  that could create blowback into mainland China, signal Beijing has lost a grip on maintaining social stability, and risk global condemnation.\par \par \uc2 "The Chinese Communist Party in its 70 years history has never allowed a protest movement to survive nor acquiesced to their public demands. So far, the protests in Hong Kong are different," says Marion Smith, executive director of non-profit Victims of Communism Memorial Foundation. He attributes that to a number of factors including Hong Kong's role as a global financial center that has been important for China's economic success, the number of international residents and media in the city covering the protest movement, and the fact that Western leaders "have made it clear that there would be significant ramifications if Beijing sent in PLA troops or enacted a bloody crackdown on the protesters."\par \par \uc2 If the escalation continues, investors will likely worry about the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar. But \uc2 Marc Chandler\uc2 , chief market strategist at Bannockburn Global \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says the situation is ultimately a political one. "As long as the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the will, the peg can withstand a stronger dollar, a recession in Hong Kong, and trade conflict between the mainland the U.S."\par \par \uc2 For investors, Hong Kong property stocks, airlines, and retailers are the most vulnerable and likely to see further pressure as the protests disrupt regular life and travel plans in Hong Kong, TS Lombard's Green says.\par \par \uc2 More important in the near-term for investors is what happens during this month's trade negotiations. In early September, U.S. Treasury Secretary \uc2 Steven Mnuchin\uc2  said Hong Kong wouldn't be part of trade discussions when the two sides meet this month. Signs from China suggest Beijing is bracing for a long struggle and slower economic growth. While China is willing to make concessions on IP protection, expanding access for foreign investment, strengthening its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and reducing the trade deficit, they aren't likely to make structural economic changes, writes TS Lombard China policy analyst Eleanor Olcott in a note.\par \par \uc2 What does that mean for a trade deal? It likely will depend on the issues the two country's leaders are facing in a couple of weeks -- potential escalation in Hong Kong for Xi and an impeachment inquiry for President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Write to Reshma Kapadia at reshma.kapadia@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 01, 2019 12:20 ET (16:20 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020191001efa1002cx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
