\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Global stocks hurt by China's trade threats, falling bond yields\b0\par\par\uc2 885 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 August 2019\par 14:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * 10-year U.S. yields below two-year for first time in 12 years\par \par \uc2 * Markets fear inversion signals recession, hope for Fed rescue\par \par \uc2 * Oil extends big overnight drop on demand, supply pressures (Updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Sujata Rao\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Aug 15 (Reuters) - China's threat to impose counter-measures in retaliation for the latest U.S. tariffs knocked stocks sprawling on Thursday, checking earlier attempt to recover from a rout sparked by fears of a world recession.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street futures erased earlier losses and were trading in positive territory, but the relentless drop in global bond yields raised fears the world economy was hurtling towards recession and weighed on global equities.\par \par \uc2 Expectations the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  and other central banks would respond robustly to the recession warning helped world stocks to steady earlier. But that recovery was cut short by the latest rhetoric from Beijing\par \par \uc2 "The only game in town is the central banks, hence the bond markets are rallying," said Peter Schaffrik, global macro strategist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We have regional bonfires in Hong Kong, Argentina, Japan against South Korea, and none of these are going away easily; each and every one is not necessarily strong enough to cause trouble."\par \par \uc2 Recession fears grew on Wednesday after yields on 10-year Treasury bonds dropped to less than two-year rates for the first time in 12 years, when the same yield curve inversion presaged the 2008 recession.\par \par \uc2 The curve has inverted before every recession in the past 50 years and sent a false signal just once.\par \par \uc2 The latest inversion has since reversed, albeit marginally, and yields on 30-year Treasuries rose off the record 1.965% low reached in Asian trade. But they are still down 60 basis points in just 12 sessions.\par \par \uc2 Markets appear to be pinning their hopes, yet again, on central banks, betting that scale of the scare would alarm policymakers, especially at the Fed. Money markets price in a growing chance the Fed will cut rates by half a point at its September meeting.\par \par \uc2 "We have seen stocks trading very poorly as a result of the yield curve inversion, so that will be flashing some additional warning lights for the Fed that they have to do more," said \uc2 Andrea Iannelli\uc2 , investment director at Fidelity International.\par \par \uc2 "The only question is, can the Fed out-dove the market? At the very least they will have to match market expectations in the short term."\par \par \uc2 The Chinese comments sent a pan-European equity index down more than half a percent and markets in London and Frankfurt lost over 1%. Earlier, Asian shares fell 0.5%. Japan's Nikkei shed 1.2% as a yen surge hit the export-heavy market.\par \par \uc2 German 30-year yields are below minus 0.2% for the first time. Ten-year yields touched a record low of minus 0.67%.\par \par \uc2 The growth worries come amid economic stress in Argentina and some other emerging markets, fears of Chinese military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  and trade tensions that show no sign of abating.\par \par \uc2 "Hoping for the best on the policy front but positioning for the worst on the economic backdrop seems to be the flavour of the day," said Stephen Innes, a managing partner at Valour Markets. "The Fed, now out of necessity alone, will need to adjust policy much more profoundly than they expected."\par \par \uc2 Not everyone buys the argument that recession is inevitable -- bond markets have been distorted by a decade of multi-trillion-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Mark Haefele, chief investment officer at UBS Global Wealth Management, said how long the curve remained inverted, and to what extent, was crucial.\par \par \uc2 "If Fed rate cuts successfully steepen the curve comfortably into positive territory, this brief curve inversion may be a premature recession signal. Neither does a yield curve inversion indicate it is time to sell equities," Haefele said.\par \par \uc2 He noted that since 1975, every curve inversion had been followed by an S&P 500 rally that lasted almost two years and delivered average gains around 40%.\par \par \uc2 SAFETY PLAYS\par \par \uc2 As the Sino-U.S. trade war escalates, long-dated bond yields have fallen across the developed world, flattening yield curves in what is considered a clear signal of a worsening growth outlook.\par \par \uc2 What sent the U.S. curve over the brink into inversion was German data on Wednesday that showed the economy had contracted in the quarter to June. That came on the heels of dire Chinese data for July.\par \par \uc2 The British yield curve also inverted. The German curve is at its flattest since 2008.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices plunged with Brent crude losing another 2% to $58.4 a barrel, after shedding 3% overnight.\par \par \uc2 Safe-haven gold was up 0.3% at $1,520 per ounce, just off recent six-year highs.\par \par \uc2 The yen was flat at 105.8 to the dollar, having earlier traded at 106.74. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gained against the dollar for eight of the past 10 sessions. Excluding a mini-crash episode in January, it recently hit 17-month highs .\par \par \uc2 The dollar index was down at 97.862, the euro up at $1.1155.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Tomo Uetake, Wayne Cole and Virginia Furness; editing by Larry King) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-8-15T13:02:19.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190815ef8f01wfy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
