\par\par \b \uc2 Spiraling Clashes Risk Pushing Hong Kong's Economy Into Recession\b0\par\par\uc2 1,626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2019\par 06:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 By John Lyons, Frances Yoon and Ese Erheriene\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG -- Pro-democracy unrest sweeping Hong Kong is threatening to tip the trading hub into recession for the first time since the global financial crisis a decade ago, as uncertainty grips an economy already buffeted by the U.S.-China trade war.\par \par \uc2 With a summer of protests showing no sign of abating, crucial industries such as tourism and retail sales are suffering as overseas visitors postpone trips and stores close amid violent conflicts. More ominously, anxiety about Hong Kong's future risks crimping long-term investment decisions, experts say.\par \par \uc2 As a result, several research houses that had forecast slight growth for the $363 billion economy in the third quarter now expect a quarter-on-quarter contraction, the second such decline in a row and a technical definition of recession. For the full year, Hong Kong's outlook has fallen by around half to 1% growth, new estimates show.\par \par \uc2 The economic damage may be permanent, observers say. Hong Kong has weathered political crises before. But this one has raised the specter of military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by mainland China, a move most believe would end \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's status as a free-market linkage point between China and the West. While the chances remain remote, the fact that it is even a possibility could deter investment for years to come.\par \par \uc2 "Right now, it looks like we're in an endless tunnel of turmoil. But even if there's a solution tomorrow, you've already seeded the idea that there could be further uncertainty down the road, and that will deter business decisions by foreign investors," said Tommy Wu, senior Asia economist at Oxford Economics in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 In recent days, Mr. Wu revised his quarter-on-quarter growth figure for the July-September period to minus-0.3%.\par \par \uc2 "We're a Hong Kong business, and like all businesses that are based here, we're feeling the impact of local political unrest," \uc2 John Slosar\uc2 , chairman of Hong Kong's flagship airline \uc2 Cathay Pacific Ltd\uc2 ., told analysts and reporters this week. He said the protests have cut inbound traffic in July and put a dent in future reservations.\par \par \uc2 "We are very concerned about the impact of the current situation on the economy and reputation of the city," said Clement Kwok, chief executive of Hongkong and Shanghai Hotels, which runs the iconic Peninsula Hong Kong in a popular harborside shopping district. The hotel had a double-digit fall in occupancy in July, its worst performance since the city was hit by severe acute respiratory syndrome, or SARS, in 2003.\par \par \uc2 The worsening economy is sure to add pressure on the city's Chief Executive \uc2 Carrie Lam\uc2 , who is facing calls for her resignation. She is blamed for sparking the protests by pushing a law to speed extradition of Hong Kong residents for trial in mainland China, enraging locals in the semiautonomous region of China. Hong Kong is grounded in values like rule of law and freedom of expression that aren't protected in the mainland.\par \par \uc2 More than two months later, some economists say Mrs. Lam missed chances to defuse the protest movement and has instead exacerbating it by refusing to acknowledge any protester demands, such as total retraction of the extradition law.\par \par \uc2 The protesters, mostly young people who see themselves as fighters for democracy in a life-or-death struggle for their futures as residents in a society rooted in rule-of-law, are unlikely to be deterred by economic concerns.\par \par \uc2 In July, the \uc2 American Chamber of Commerce\uc2  In Hong Kong said the crisis had hurt member businesses and instilled anxiety over the future. In a poll, a majority of members called on Hong Kong leaders to drop the controversial extradition bill that sparked the protests and meet other protester demands.\par \par \uc2 "We are now in the worst-case scenario, where the government chooses to toughen its stance on protesters," said \uc2 Kevin Lai\uc2 , a Hong Kong-based economist at Daiwa Capital Markets. Mr. Lai now forecasts that the economy will shrink to minus-0.8% in the third quarter from the previous quarter, and he called a recession inevitable.\par \par \uc2 On Friday Mrs. Lam, who retains support from Beijing, said the economic impact had been worse than the 2003 SARS epidemic and blamed it on violent protesters. She vowed to take measures to boost the economy and protect Hong Kong's image.\par \par \uc2 "This economic downturn is very fast, some people have described it as a coming tsunami," Mrs. Lam said.\par \par \uc2 At a separate news conference, Finance Secretary Paul Chan said, "The economic situations, both domestically and externally, are challenging. If the weakness continues in the third quarter of this year, we'll technically enter into a recession."\par \par \uc2 Officials from the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  have sought to send out reassuring signals regarding possible risks such as capital flight and short selling against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , citing experience, high capital requirements and massive reserves.,\par \par \uc2 The MSCI Hong Kong index has fallen around 9% since the protests began in June.\par \par \uc2 Economically, the timing couldn't be worse. With around half of its trade passing through mainland China, Hong Kong is reeling from the U.S.-China trade war. Its economy barely avoided recession in 2018 as import-export volumes fell nearly 8%.\par \par \uc2 Now, retail sales that have slipped for months are plunging, falling nearly 7% in June, the first month of protests. Figures aren't available yet for July, when protests escalated in number and violence.\par \par \uc2 But the view isn't good from the streets.\par \par \uc2 On a recent Saturday afternoon, protesters flooded through one of Hong Kong's toniest shopping districts, a strip of luxury brands such as Gucci and \uc2 Louis Vuitton\uc2  that are a main attraction for tourists, often mainland Chinese who flock to Hong Kong for weekend shopping jaunts. Shoppers, some wheeling purchases in carry-on style luggage, stared blankly as protesters marched through. Traffic snarled and some shops closed.\par \par \uc2 Mainland Chinese make up 40% of Hong Kong's tourism but 80% of its retail sales, according to the \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  research firm. Widespread news coverage of the anti-Beijing protests appear to be crimping their desire to travel and spend in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "Chinese tourist spending in our shops has declined," Andre Hoffmann, managing director of L'Occitane Far East Ltd., told a recent earnings conference call. It's "a bad cocktail for our business."\par \par \uc2 French cosmetics giant \uc2 L'Occitane International SA\uc2  announced last week that revenue from Hong Kong fell 19% in the three months ending in June, in part because stores had to close or were empty of customers on several days due to the protests.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, Italian luxury brand Prada told investors that unrest hurt sales in Hong Kong even as sales in mainland China improved.\par \par \uc2 "Some customers were surprised when an announcement went off saying we were closing the stores early that weekend because protests were happening near us," said a Prada salesperson at a store popular with mainland Chinese tourists, recalling a recent day protests surged outside.\par \par \uc2 "They were caught off guard and were asking us what was going on. We told them that they had to leave the store immediately."\par \par \uc2 "People are not in the mood to shop these days. The atmosphere around our mall has been pretty quiet," said an attendant at the luxury shoe brand \uc2 Giuseppe Zanotti\uc2  in a high-end shopping mall in downtown Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Hong Kong Commerce Secretary \uc2 Edward Yau\uc2  said tourist arrivals had plunged 31% in the first week of August compared with last year. More than 20 countries including the U.S. and Australia have issued travel advisories.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the protest movement is only escalating. After a general strike on Monday, Aug. 5, police fired 800 rounds of tear gas to contain multiple demonstrations -- nearly as much as was deployed in the previous two months of mass protests.\par \par \uc2 The strike disrupted transportation across the city and led to hundreds of flight cancellations at the airport.\par \par \uc2 Across Hong Kong, multinational and financial companies are making contingency plans for further unrest. Security experts are holding seminars on what to do if caught in a protest, and employees are working from home when unrest is expected. Persuading executives to relocate to Hong Kong has become trickier, business consultants say.\par \par \uc2 At Asian Tigers Mobility, a relocation service that helps move global executives to and from Hong Kong, summer business is way down even though it is normally the busiest time, in most years requiring the company to add extra staff, said a person familiar with Asian Tigers.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. corporate client relocated one employee before the protests started but is delaying sending three others -- and instead considering sending them to Singapore. "It is getting to the point now where I think people thought it was going to blow over. It hasn't blown over," the person said.\par \par \uc2 The unrest has raised uncertainty for businesses big and small.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Ching, a 36-year-old Australian who opened two Vietnamese lunch restaurants in recent years, supports the ideals behind the protests and even gave out free sandwiches at a peaceful march. But as the protests became violent, he has had to close shop several times, squeezing his tight margins and making him wonder whether he should leave.\par \par \uc2 "Any prudent person would be putting plans in place, and myself and other expats and other business owners are doing the same," he said.\par \par \uc2 --Joanne Chiu and Natasha Khan contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to John Lyons at john.lyons@wsj.com, Frances Yoon at frances.yoon@wsj.com and Ese Erheriene at ese.erheriene@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 12-08-19 0413GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020190812ef8c0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
