amended standards adopted by the Group (Continued) \par  \par        (1)     HKFRS 9, "Financial instruments" (Continued) \par  \par        The following table reconciles the prior period's closing impairment allowance measured in accordance with the \par        HKAS 39 incurred loss model to the new impairment allowance measured in accordance with the HKFRS 9 \par        expected loss model at 1 January 2018: \par  \par        HK$'000 \par                                                                 Impairment                                 Impairment \par                                                                   allowance                                  allowance \par                                                                       under                                      under \par        Measurement category                                       HKAS 39          Remeasurement             HKFRS 9 \par  \par        Cash and balances with banks                                        -                     731                 731 \par        Placements with banks maturing between \par          one and twelve months                                             -                   3,284               3,284 \par        Financial assets at fair value through other \par          comprehensive income                                            -                   17,602              17,602 \par        Financial assets at amortised cost                                -                    5,632               5,632 \par        Trade bills                                                  14,229                  (11,945)              2,284 \par        Advances to customers                                       659,046                  299,354             958,400 \par        Accrued interest and other accounts                          11,431                    6,670              18,101 \par        Loan commitments and financial guarantees \par                                                                            -                140,456             140,456 \par                                                                    684,706                  461,784           1,146,490 \par        Total \par  \par  \par        HKFRS 9 outlines a "three-stage" model for impairment based on changes in credit quality since initial \par        recognition as summarised below: \par  \par                A financial instrument that is not credit-impaired on initial recognition is classified in "Stage 1" and \par                 has its credit risk continuously monitored by the Group. \par                If a significant increase in credit risk since initial recognition is identified, the financial instrument is \par                 moved to "Stage 2" but is not yet deemed to be credit-impaired. \par                If the financial instrument is credit-impaired, the financial instrument is then moved to "Stage 3". \par                Financial instruments in Stage 1 have their ECL measured at an amount equal to the portion of lifetime \par                 expected credit losses that result from default events possible within the next 12 months. Instruments in \par                 Stages 2 or 3 have their ECL measured based on expected credit losses on a lifetime basis. \par                A pervasive concept in measuring ECL in accordance with HKFRS 9 is that it should consider forward \par                 looking information. \par                Purchased or originated credit-impaired financial assets are those financial assets that are credit \par                 impaired on initial recognition. Their ECL is always measured on a lifetime basis (Stage 3). \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                             7 \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dah Sing Banking Group Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par  \par  \par 2.     Basis of Preparation and Accounting Policies (Continued) \par  \par        (a)      New and amended standards adopted by the Group (Continued) \par  \par                 HKFRS 15, "Revenue from contracts with customers" \par        (2) \par  \par        The HKICPA has issued HKFRS 15 as the new standard for the recognition of revenue, which has become \par  \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 27 Mar 2019 00:08 ET \par HK Bourse: Results Announcement From \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dah Sing Banking Group Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  . -4-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par        effective for financial years commencing on or after 1 January 2018. This replaces HKAS 18 which covers \par        contracts for goods and services and HKAS 11 which covers construction contracts. The new standard is based \par        on the principle that revenue is recognised when control of a good or service transfers to a customer. The \par        standard permits either a full retrospective or a modified retrospective approach for the adoption. \par  \par        The adoption of HKFRS 15 does not have any material impact on the Group's consolidated financial statements. \par  \par        (b)      New standards and interpretations not yet adopted \par  \par        A number of new standards and amendments to standards and interpretations are effective for annual periods \par        beginning after 1 January 2018, and have not been early adopted in preparing these consolidated financial \par        statements. Key changes of the new standards are summarised as follows: \par  \par                 HKFRS 16, "Leases" \par        (1) \par  \par        HKFRS 16 will result in almost all leases being recognised on the balance sheet, as the distinction between \par        operating and finance leases is removed. Under the new standard, an asset (the right to use the leased item) and a \par        financial liability to pay rentals are recognised. The only exceptions are short-term and low-value leases. Some \par        of the commitments may be covered by the exception for short-term and low value leases and some \par        commitments may relate to arrangements that will not qualify as leases under HKFRS 16. \par  \par        The accounting for lessors will not significantly change. \par  \par        The standard will affect primarily the accounting for Group's operating leases. The implementation is expected \par        to increase assets and liabilities by approximately HK$500 million. \par  \par        The new standard is mandatory for financial years commencing on or after 1 January 2019. The Group also \par        intends to apply the simplified transition approach and will not restate comparative amounts for the year prior to \par        first adoption. \par  \par        There are no other HKFRSs or interpretations that are effective from 1 January 2018 or not yet effective that \par        would be expected to have a material impact on the Group. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                            8 \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dah Sing Banking Group Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par  \par  \par 3.     Operating segment reporting \par  \par        Segment reporting by the Group is prepared in accordance with HKFRS 8 "Operating Segments". Information \par        reported to the chief operating decision maker, including the Chief Executive and other Executive Committee \par        members, for the purposes of resource allocation and performance assessment, is determined on the basis of \par        personal banking, commercial banking, treasury and overseas banking business. Operating performances are \par        analysed by business activities for local banking business, and on business entity basis for overseas banking \par        business. \par  \par        Considering the customer groups, products and services of local businesses, the economic environment and \par        regulations, the Group splits the operating segments of the Group into the following reportable segments: \par  \par           Personal banking business includes the acceptance of deposits from individual customers and the extension \par            of residential mortgage lending, personal loans, overdraft, vehicle financing and credit card services and the \par            provision of insurance sales and investment services. \par  \par           Commercial banking business includes the acceptance of deposits from and the advance of loans and \par            working capital finance to commercial, industrial and institutional customers, and the provision of trade \par            financing. \par  \par           Treasury activities are mainly the provision of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  services and centralised cash management \par            for deposit taking and lending, interest rate risk management, management of investment in securities and \par            the overall funding of the Group. \par  \par           Overseas banking businesses include personal banking, commercial banking business activities provided by \par            overseas subsidiaries in Macau and China, and the Group's interest in a commercial bank in China. \par  \par           Others include results of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  not directly identified under other reportable segments, corporate \par            investments and debt funding (including subordinated notes). \par  \par        For the purpose of segment reporting, revenue derived from customers, products and services directly \par        identifiable with individual segments are reported directly under respective segments, while revenue and \par        funding cost arising from inter-segment funding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and funding resources are allocated to segments by \par  \par        way of transfer pricing mechanism with reference to market interest rates. Transactions within segments are \par        priced based on similar terms offered to or transacted with external parties. Inter-segment income or expenses \par        are eliminated on consolidation. \par  \par        All direct costs incurred by different segments are grouped under respective segments. Indirect costs and \par        support functions' costs are allocated to various segments and products based on effort and time spent as well as \par        segments' operating income depending on the nature of costs incurred. Costs related to corporate activities that \par        cannot be reasonably allocated to segments, products and support functions are grouped under Others as \par        unallocated corporate expenses. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                           9 \par   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dah Sing Banking Group Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par  \par  \par   3.           Operating segment reporting (Continued) \par  \par                For the year ended 31 December 2018 \par  \par                                        Personal    Commercial                    Overseas                      Inter- \par HK$'000                                Banking        Banking     Treasury        Banking       Others       segment          Total \par  \par  \par Net interest income                   1,742,431     1,238,085      625,952        543,458       14,165              -     4,164,091 \par Non-interest income/ (expenses)        938,523        204,650       (70,558)      142,890      351,326          (996)     1,565,835 \par  \par  \par Total operating income                2,680,954     1,442,735      555,394        686,348      365,491          (996)     5,729,926 \par Operating expenses                   (1,580,816)     (484,847)     (159,777)     (491,678)      15,827           996      (2,700,295) \par  \par  \par Operating profit before credit \par   impairment (losses)/ written \par   back                                1,100,138       957,888      395,617        194,670      381,318              -     3,029,631 \par Credit impair