per share for 2018 to shareholders whose names are on the Register of \par Shareholders as at the close of business on Tuesday, 4 June 2019. Subject to shareholders' approval at the AGM, the \par final dividend will be payable on Wednesday, 12 June 2019. \par  \par CLOSURE OF REGISTER OF SHAREHOLDERS \par  \par For determining shareholders' right to attend and vote at the AGM: \par  \par Closure dates of Register of Shareholders                                                         21 May 2019 (Tuesday) \par  (both days inclusive)                                                                           to 24 May 2019 (Friday) \par Latest time to lodge transfers                                                       4:30 p.m. on 20 May 2019 (Monday) \par Record date                                                                                         24 May 2019 (Friday) \par AGM                                                                                                 24 May 2019 (Friday) \par  \par For determining shareholders' entitlement to receive the proposed final dividend: \par  \par Closure dates of Register of Shareholders                                                           31 May 2019 (Friday) \par  (both days inclusive)                                                                          to 4 June 2019 (Tuesday) \par Latest time to lodge transfers                                                      4:30 p.m. on 30 May 2019 (Thursday) \par Record date                                                                                        4 June 2019 (Tuesday) \par  \par During the periods of the closure of Register of Shareholders, no share transfers will be registered. For registration, all \par transfer documents accompanied by the relevant share certificates must be lodged with the Company's share registrar, \par Computershare Hong Kong Investor Services Limited, Shops 1712-1716, 17th Floor, Hopewell Centre, 183 Queen's \par Road East, Wan Chai, Hong Kong before the relevant latest time to lodge transfers. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                                41 \par Dah Sing Banking Group Limited \par  \par  \par CORPORATE AND BUSINESS OVERVIEW \par  \par HIGHLIGHTS \par  \par Overall, whilst growth slowed somewhat in 2018, it was still generally a good year for the Hong Kong economy. GDP \par growth was 3.0%, down from 3.8% in 2017, but still above the trend growth averaging at 2.8% over the past 10 years. \par  \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 27 Mar 2019 00:11 ET \par HK Bourse: Results Announcement From Dah Sing Banking Group Ltd. -16-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par Imports and exports both grew during the year, albeit at a slightly slower pace than in 2017, and overall trade growth was \par a respectable 7.9% for the year. The performance of retail sales was particularly strong, with growth of 8.8%, much \par stronger than the 2.2% reported in 2017, and due in part to the increase in visitor numbers from 58.5 million to 65.1 \par million. Unemployment remained very low at 2.8%, whilst inflation increased from 1.5% to 2.4% year on year. \par  \par Globally, conditions were steady, with the IMF forecasting global GDP growth for 2018 of 3.7%, the same as in 2017. \par Economic growth in the Mainland slowed from 6.9% in 2017 to 6.6% for the year, and the RMB weakened during the \par year, particularly in the first half. The US economy continued to recover, with GDP growth of 2.9% for the year, \par compared with 2.3% in the prior year. Employment figures also improved, with unemployment falling to 3.9% by the \par year end, from 4.1% at the beginning of the year. The European Union's economic growth slowed during the year, \par falling to 1.4% in the fourth quarter of the year. \par  \par The US Federal Reserve raised the Fed Funds rate four times in 2018, and market interest rates generally followed suit, \par although towards the year end, expectations for further rate increases in 2019 began to moderate. Hong Kong market \par interest rates were volatile, generally remaining below US interest rates, reflecting continued strong liquidity in the Hong \par Kong domestic market, and from time to time trading significantly below US dollar interest rates, resulting in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong \par Kong Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  having to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar exchange rate reached the weak end \par of the range of the linked exchange rate mechanism of 7.85 to the US dollar. \par  \par With satisfactory market conditions, albeit with a slowing economy particularly in the second half of the year, our \par operating profit after credit impairment losses increased by 19.1% to HK$2,796 million. Due to the write-down of our \par investment in Bank of Chongqing ("BOCQ") by HK$633 million in total in 2018 as a result of a lower Value in Use (see \par below), our profit attributable to shareholders was up by 13.4% to HK$2,480 million. \par  \par BUSINESS AND FINANCIAL REVIEW \par  \par Key revenue lines all improved, with net interest income up by 7.0% to HK$4,164 million, mainly due to volume growth, \par with our net interest margin at 2.01%. Net fee and commission income grew by 10.3% to HK$1,161 million due mainly \par to the growth in our wealth management business during the year. Despite the adoption of a new accounting standard, \par HKFRS 9, relating to credit impairment starting January 2018, credit impairment losses dropped slightly during the year \par to HK$233 million. \par  \par The Value in Use of our investment in BOCQ was again below the carrying value of the investment as at 31 December \par 2018, resulting in the need for a further impairment charge of HK$230 million at the year end, which together with the \par impairment charge of HK$403 million made at the mid-year, resulted in a total impairment charge for the year of \par HK$633 million, lower than 2017 by HK$182 million, or 22%. Despite the need for an impairment charge to reduce the \par carrying value of the investment, the operating performance of BOCQ remained satisfactory, and our share of its profit \par was HK$660 million for the year, around 5% higher than the prior year. Our wholly owned subsidiaries, Banco \par Comercial de Macau and Dah Sing Bank (China), continued to deliver a steady performance. \par  \par As mentioned above, credit costs overall were down slightly for the year, led mainly by decreases in loan impairments in \par our Hong Kong and Mainland based commercial banking businesses. Credit impairment charges were, however, higher \par in the second half of the year. The credit quality in our retail banking business remained satisfactory throughout the year, \par although reported some increase in credit impairment charges due mainly to volume increases. \par  \par Measuring on the net profit excluding the impairment charge on BOCQ, the underlying return on assets of 1.4% and \par return of shareholders' funds of 12.3% were slightly below 2017. Our cost to income ratio improved from 49.0% to \par 47.1% during the year, a result of stronger rate of growth in operating income and continued efforts on cost control. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                             42 \par Dah Sing Banking Group Limited \par  \par  \par BUSINESS AND FINANCIAL REVIEW (CONTINUED) \par  \par As at 31 December 2018, the consolidated Common Equity Tier 1 ratio of Dah Sing Bank, Limited ("DSB") was 13.1%, \par slightly lower than 13.4% at the end of 2017. Taking into account DSB's outstanding Tier 2 subordinated debts, DSB's \par consolidated capital adequacy level at the year end was 18.1%, slightly lower than the prior year, due partly to the \par  \par amortisation of the capital eligibility of certain previously issued Tier 2 subordinated debts. We continued to operate \par well above the minimum Liquidity Maintenance Ratio of 25%, reporting an average level of 46% during the year. We \par believe that our capital and liquidity ratios are maintained at levels within the market range for both peer banks in Hong \par Kong, and indeed for banks internationally. \par  \par PROSPECTS \par  \par We entered 2019 with a number of uncertainties, notably, the possible impact of a trade war, and particularly the impact \par of higher tariffs and restrictions on trade with the Mainland by the US. These developments, amongst others, unsettled \par equity investors in Hong Kong, resulting in a much weaker equity market in Hong Kong in late 2018 and early 2019. \par Moreover, it is also difficult to gauge the implications of Brexit on the global economy. \par  \par All of this makes us somewhat more cautious on the outlook for 2019. However, currently our overall business \par conditions remain relatively stable, so we are not at this stage unduly pessimistic, and we expect to continue to see at \par least modest loan growth in 2019. The potential impact of a Sino-US trade war has made us somewhat less positive about \par the credit outlook, particularly for our commercial banking business, although there are currently some signs that the \par trade war may be downplayed or even resolved before too long. However, we have considered this uncertain and less \par favourable outlook, and made sufficient credit impairment provisions at the end of 2018. Impairment charges, barring \par exceptional market developments, are expected to be manageable. \par  \par Expectations for US interest rate rises have moderated, and this is likely to mean that Hong Kong interest rates also \par remain somewhat lower than initially expected. However, the current substantial differential in rates between US dollars \par and HK dollars may put some pressure on our asset yields, and this could have some impact on our net interest margin \par for the year. Pressure on expenses is expected to remain manageable, although investments in our IT systems and \par infrastructure are becoming more demanding. \par  \par Our capital adequacy remains robust, and after the new issuance of US$225 million of Basel III compliant Tier 2 \par subordinated debt in January, we expect our capital position to remain adequate for the remaining part of 2019. Liquidity \par in the Hong Kong market also remains generally robust, and our funding base, mainly comprising domestic Hong Kong \par deposits is also expected to remain stable. \par  \par Whilst we are still somewhat cautious over the outlook in 2019, we continue to believe that there are opportunities to \par continue to grow our businesses, and to provide a greater range and depth of products and services to our retail and \par commercial customers. We are excited by the developments in the Greater Bay Area, where we will strive to grow with \par our customers to capture new and increasing business and market opportunities, with our presence in Hong Kong, \par Macau, Shenzhen and Guangzhou, the key cities in the region. \par  \par COMPLIANCE WITH THE CORPORATE GOVERNANCE CODE \par  \par The Company has applied the principles and complied with all the code provisions set out in the Corporate Governance \par Code ("CG Code") contained in Appendix 14 of the Listing Rules throughout the year ended 31 December 2018, with \par the exception of code provision A.4.1 and code provision E.1.2. \par  \par Pursuant to code provision A.4.1 of the CG Code, non-executive directors should be appointed for a specific term, \par subject to re-election. The Non-Executive Directors of the Company are not appointed for a specific term, but are subject \par to retirement by rotation and re-election at annual general meetings in accordance with the provisions of the Company's \par Articles of Association. \par  \par Pursuant to code provision E.1.2 of the CG Code, the chairman of the board should attend the annual general meeting. \par Mr. David Shou-Yeh Wong, the Chairman of the Board, was unable to attend the 2018 Annual General Meeting (the \par "2018 AGM") of the Company held on 25 May 2018 due to a minor physical indisposition. Mr. Hon-Hing Wong (Derek \par Wong), Vice Chairman, Managing Director and Chief Executive of the Company, took the chair of the 2018 AGM. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                            43 \par Dah Sing Banking Group Limited \par  \par  \par CODE FOR SECURITIES TRANSACTIONS BY DIRECTORS \par  \par The Company has adopted its own code of conduct for directors' securities dealing ("Directors' Dealing Code") on terms \par no less exacting than the prevailing required standard set out in the Model Code for Securities Transactions by Directors \par of Listed Issuers ("Model Code") under Appendix 10 of the Listing Rules. Following specific enquiry, the Directors of \par the Company confirmed that they had complied with the required standard set out in the Model Code and the Directors' \par  \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 27 Mar 2019 00:11 ET \par HK Bourse: Results Announcement From Dah Sing Banking Group Ltd. -17-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par Dealing Code throughout the year ended 31 December 2018. \par  \par AUDIT COMMITTEE \par  \par The Audit Committee has reviewed with Management the accounting principles and practices adopted by the Group and \par discussed internal controls and financial reporting matters including a review of this financial report and the consolidated \par financial statements of the Group for the year ended 31 December 2018. \par  \par PURCHASE, SALE OR REDEMPTION OF SECURITIES \par  \par There was no purchase, sale or redemption by the Company, or any of its subsidiaries, of the listed securities of the \par Company during the year ended 31 December 2018. \par  \par ANNUAL RESULTS ANNOUNCEMENT AND ANNUAL REPORT \par  \par This announcement is published on the websites of the Hong Kong Exchanges and Clearing Limited ("HKEX") at \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.hkexnews.hk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.hkexnews.hk}}}\uc2 \f2  and Dah Sing Bank at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dahsing.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.dahsing.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par The 2018 Annual Report of the Group containing all the information required by the Listing Rules will be published on \par the websites of HKEX and Dah Sing Bank in due course. Printed copies of the 2018 Annual Report will be sent to \par shareholders who have elected to receive printed versions of the Company's corporate communications before the end of \par April 2019. \par  \par BOARD OF DIRECTORS \par  \par As at the date of this announcement, the Board of Directors of the Company comprises Messrs. David Shou-Yeh Wong \par (Chairman), Hon-Hing Wong (Derek Wong) (Vice Chairman, Managing Director and Chief Executive), Gary Pak-Ling \par Wang (Group Chief Financial and Operating Officer) and Nicholas John Mayhew (Deputy Chief Executive) as Executive \par Directors; Mr. Jun Fujimoto as Non-Executive Director; Messrs. Robert Tsai-To Sze, Seng-Lee Chan, Yuen-Tin Ng and \par Blair Chilton Pickerell as Independent Non-Executive Directors. \par  \par  \par  \par Source: Hong Kong Exchanges & Clearing \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 27, 2019 00:11 ET (04:11 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190327ef3r0002g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
