ed loss model at 1 January 2018: \par  \par        HK$'000 \par                                                                Impairment                                    Impairment \par                                                                  allowance                                     allowance \par                                                                      under                                         under \par            Measurement category                                  HKAS 39            Remeasurement              HKFRS 9 \par  \par            Cash and balances with banks                                     -                   1,028                1,028 \par            Placements with banks maturing between \par              one and twelve months                                          -                   4,134                4,134 \par            Financial assets at fair value through other \par              comprehensive income                                         -                    17,738              17,738 \par            Financial assets at amortised cost                             -                     5,777               5,777 \par            Trade bills                                               14,229                   (11,945)              2,284 \par            Advances to customers                                    659,046                  299,354              958,400 \par            Accrued interest and other accounts                       24,595                     7,059              31,654 \par            Loan commitments and financial \par              guarantees                                                     -                140,456              140,456 \par                                                                     697,870                  463,601            1,161,471 \par            Total \par  \par  \par        HKFRS 9 outlines a "three-stage" model for impairment based on changes in credit quality since initial \par        recognition as summarised below: \par  \par                   A financial instrument that is not credit-impaired on initial recognition is classified in "Stage 1" and \par                    has its credit risk continuously monitored by the Group. \par                   If a significant increase in credit risk since initial recognition is identified, the financial instrument is \par                    moved to "Stage 2" but is not yet deemed to be credit-impaired. \par                   If the financial instrument is credit-impaired, the financial instrument is then moved to "Stage 3". \par                   Financial instruments in Stage 1 have their ECL measured at an amount equal to the portion of \par                    lifetime expected credit losses that result from default events possible within the next 12 months. \par                    Instruments in Stages 2 or 3 have their ECL measured based on expected credit losses on a lifetime \par                    basis. \par                   A pervasive concept in measuring ECL in accordance with HKFRS 9 is that it should consider \par                    forward looking information. \par                   Purchased or originated credit-impaired financial assets are those financial assets that are credit \par                    impaired on initial recognition. Their ECL is always measured on a lifetime basis (Stage 3). \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                                7 \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dah Sing Financial Holdings Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par  \par  \par 2.     Basis of Preparation and Accounting Policies (Continued) \par  \par        (a)      New and amended standards adopted by the Group (Continued) \par  \par                 HKFRS 15, "Revenue from contracts with customers" \par        (2) \par  \par        The HKICPA has issued HKFRS 15 as the new standard for the recognition of revenue, which has become \par        effective for financial years commencing on or after 1 January 2018. This replaces HKAS 18 which covers \par        contracts for goods and services and HKAS 11 which covers construction contracts. The new standard is \par        based on the principle that revenue is recognised when control of a good or service transfers to a customer. \par  \par        The standard permits either a full retrospective or a modified retrospective approach for the adoption. \par  \par        The adoption of HKFRS 15 does not have any material impact on the Group's consolidated financial \par        statements. \par  \par        (b)      New standards and interpretations not yet adopted \par  \par        A number of new standards and amendments to standards and interpretations are effective for annual periods \par        beginning after 1 January 2018, and have not been early adopted in preparing these consolidated financial \par        statements. Key changes of the new standards are summarised as follows: \par  \par                 HKFRS 16, "Leases" \par        (1) \par  \par        HKFRS 16 will result in almost all leases being recognised on the balance sheet, as the distinction between \par        operating and finance leases is removed. Under the new standard, an asset (the right to use the leased item) \par        and a financial liability to pay rentals are recognised. The only exceptions are short-term and low-value \par        leases. Some of the commitments may be covered by the exception for short-term and low value leases and \par        some commitments may relate to arrangements that will not qualify as leases under HKFRS 16. \par  \par        The accounting for lessors will not significantly change. \par  \par        The standard will affect primarily the accounting for Group's operating leases. The implementation is \par        expected to increase assets and liabilities by approximately HK$500 million. \par  \par        The new standard is mandatory for financial years commencing on or after 1 January 2019. The Group also \par        intends to apply the simplified transition approach and will not restate comparative amounts for the year prior \par        to first adoption. \par  \par                 HKFRS 17, "Insurance contracts" \par        (2) \par  \par        In January 2018, the HKICPA issued HKFRS 17, a new accounting standard for insurance contracts covering \par        recognition and measurement, presentation and disclosure, which replaces HKFRS 4 "Insurance contracts". \par  \par        In contrast to the requirements in HKFRS 4, which are largely based on grandfathering previous local \par        accounting policies for measurement purposes, HKFRS 17 provides a comprehensive model (the general \par        model) for insurance contracts, supplemented by the variable fee approach for contracts with direct \par        participation features that are substantially investment-related service contracts, and the premium allocation \par        approach mainly for short-duration contracts which typically applies to certain non-life insurance contracts. \par  \par        HKFRS 17 is effective for annual reporting periods beginning on or after 1 January 2021. The Group is yet to \par        assess the impact of the standard on the Group's consolidated financial statements. \par  \par        There are no other HKFRSs or interpretations that are effective from 1 January 2018 or not yet effective that \par        would be expected to have a material impact on the Group. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                          8 \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dah Sing Financial Holdings Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par  \par  \par 3.     Operating segment reporting \par  \par        Segment reporting by the Group is prepared in accordance with HKFRS 8 "Operating Segments". \par        Information reported to the chief operating decision maker, including the Chief Executive and other \par        Executive Committee members, for the purposes of resource allocation and performance assessment, is \par        determined on the basis of banking business and insurance business. For banking business, operating \par        performances are analysed by business activities for local banking business, and on business entity basis for \par        overseas banking business. For insurance business, resources allocation and performance evaluation are \par        based on insurance business entity basis. \par  \par        Considering the customer groups, products and services of local businesses, the economic environment and \par        regulations, the Group splits the operating segments of the Group into the following reportable segments: \par  \par                Personal banking business includes the acceptance of deposits from individual customers and the \par                 extension of residential mortgage lending, personal loans, overdraft, vehicle financing and credit \par                 card services, and the provision of insurance sales and investment services. \par  \par                Commercial banking business includes the acceptance of deposits from and the advance of loans and \par                 working capital finance to commercial, industrial and institutional customers, and the provision of \par                 trade financing. \par  \par                Treasury activities are mainly the provision of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  services and centralised cash \par                 management for deposit taking and lending, interest rate risk management, management of \par  \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 27 Mar 2019 00:12 ET \par HK Bourse: Results Announcement From \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dah Sing Financial Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  . -5-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par                 investment in securities and the overall funding of the Group. \par  \par                Overseas banking businesses include personal banking, commercial banking business activities \par                 provided by overseas subsidiaries in Macau and China, and the Group's interest in a commercial \par                 bank in China. \par  \par                Insurance business includes the Group's insurance and pension fund management business. Through \par                 the Group's wholly-owned subsidiaries in Hong Kong and 96% owned subsidiaries in Macau, the \par                 Group offers a variety of insurance products and services. \par  \par                Others include results of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  not directly identified under other reportable segments, \par                 corporate investments and debt funding (including subordinated notes). \par  \par        For the purpose of segment reporting, revenue derived from customers, products and services directly \par        identifiable with individual segments are reported directly under respective segments, while revenue and \par        funding cost arising from inter-segment funding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and funding resources are allocated to segments by \par        way of transfer pricing mechanism with reference to market interest rates. Transactions within segments are \par        priced based on similar terms offered to or transacted with external parties. Inter-segment income or \par        expenses are eliminated on consolidation. \par  \par        All direct costs incurred by different segments are grouped under respective segments. Indirect costs and \par        support functions' costs are allocated to various segments and products based on effort and time spent as well \par        as segments' operating income depending on the nature of costs incurred. Costs related to corporate activities \par        that cannot be reasonably allocated to segments, products and support functions are grouped under Others as \par        unallocated corporate expenses. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                         9 \par                 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dah Sing Financial Holdings Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par  \par  \par                 3.               Operating segment reporting (Continued) \par  \par                                  For the year ended 31 December 2018 \par  \par                                             Personal    Commercial                        Overseas    Insurance                     Inter- \par HK$'000                                     Banking        Banking     Treasury           Banking      Business       Others      segment             Total \par  \par  \par  \par Net interest income                        1,742,431      1,238,085     625,952            543,458       28,489       36,577            -         4,214,992 \par Non-interest income/ (expenses)             938,523        204,650       (70,558)          142,890      124,176      367,181       (15,924)       1,690,938 \par  \par  \par  \par Total operating income net of \par  insurance claims                          2,680,954      1,442,735     555,394            686,348      152,665      403,758       (15,924)       5,905,930 \par  \par Operating expenses                        (1,580,816)     (484,847)     (159,777)         (491,678)    (117,999)        6,798       15,924       (2,812,395) \par  \par  \par  \par Operating profit before credit \par  \par  impairment (losses)/ written back         1,100,138       957,888      395,617            194,670       34,666      410,556                -     3,093,535 \par  \par Credit impairment (losses)/ written \par  back                                      (240,734)        17,242        (5,188)           (5,703)        (123)        1,458               -      (233,048) \par  \par  \par  \par Operating profit after credit \par  \par  impairment (losses)/ written back          859,404        975,130      390,429            188,967       34,543      412,