\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  Again to Support HK Dollar -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 August 2018\par 12:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1032 GMT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's de-facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought more Hong Kong dollars in Asian trading hours to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg with the greenback. The latest purchase of US$300 million worth of Hong Kong dollars by the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes after it bought $275 million in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars during US trading hours Tuesday, the first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since May. That move will reduce the sum of balances on clearing accounts maintained by banks with the \uc2 HKMA\uc2  to HK$104.89 billion ($13.36 billion) on August 17. During April and May, \uc2 HKMA\uc2  bought a combined HK$70.35 billion to defend the city's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (joanne.chiu@wsj.com ; @joannechiuhk)\par \par \uc2 1031 GMT - Turkish banks have a "high level of probability to work out a non-performing loan problem" due to a "very high level of profitability" says \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  . This is because Turkish banks make pre-provisioning profits of 4.5% of total loans each year, it says. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says that the issue for Turkish banks is that Turkey's non-performing loans (NPLs) ratio, currently at 4.5%, could end up rising to levels seen in a normal emerging market recession or crisis of around 20%. This would equate to about 40% of Turkish banks' CET1. Turkish banks' high profitability levels combined with lower leverage ratios of around 10 times would make Turkish banks resilient to a recession-like NPLs ratio, says \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  . (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1007 GMT - More and more people have become concerned that the U.K. will exit from the \uc2 European Union\uc2  without a deal on March 31 next year, says BNY Mellon. This is "a point reflected in the current political betting with odds of 4/6 being offered on this outcome." But the U.K. could also ask for an extension to the Article 50 to have another general election and choose a government which would be able to agree on what kind of a relationship it wants with the EU . Or it could hold another Brexit referendum. None of these scenarios that BNY Mellon sees possible include a soft Brexit option. The pound has been falling due to these uncertainties. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0958 GMT - The 2.5% rise in U.K. inflation in July "represents a peak," ING analysts say, adding they expect inflation "to begin trending downwards over the next few months gradually." Oil prices, which were behind the rise, are expected to fall. Moreover, "the impact of the pound's post-Brexit plunge is continuing to fade." This means that the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  will "remain on the sidelines for the next few months, as Brexit uncertainty becomes an increasing concern." (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0942 GMT - Despite concerns about further weakening in the Chinese yuan, \uc2 Schroders\uc2 ' Shanghai CIO Jack Lee doesn't expect a drastic depreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the prospect of major capital outflows isn't something Beijing wants to see. "It's probably not right to be overly bearish" on the yuan, he contends while highlighting the recent move by Beijing to reimpose a requirement that makes it more expensive to bet against the yuan. Meanwhile, he says the recent weakness in Chinese tech stocks mean the sector is "entering into a more-maturing stage" after a half-dozen years of growth driven by constant innovation and rising penetration rates. (joanne.chiu@wsj.com; @joannechiuhk)\par \par \uc2 0935 GMT - This year's rate hikes from \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  may not be done, says \uc2 OCBC\uc2 , as the Fed is poised to continue its cycle of increases. That as "the prospect of further trade tensions between the US and China or the US with other countries could further" dampen sentiment towards the rupiah, which hit 3-year lows versus the dollar this week. That as the investment bank is predicting 5.1% GDP growth for Indonesia this year, the lower end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's forecast. (i-made.sentana@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0911 GMT - Unlike previous easing cycles in which China's policy makers stimulated both infrastructure and property investment, this time around Beijing will likely shun the latter--at least initially--says \uc2 Nomura\uc2 . The investment bank contends that apart from a changing mentality toward financial risks, a lack of consensus on the best strategies among the top leadership may also result in a different policy path this go-around. It thinks Beijing will probably keep putting deleveraging on hold until it "feels the imminent risk of financial and growth instability is cleared." Property investment has been holding up this year despite a sharp slowdown in overall investment, official data show. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0904 GMT - Maybank pegs the next resistance level for the greenback at S$1.3860 as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to hit 1-year highs. As the dollar continues to rise amid risk-off sentiment across assets, it broke above S$1.38 today. Its currently right at that level, and Maybank puts support at S$1.3760. Locally, eyes are on Friday's July exports data. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0901 GMT -- \uc2 Norway's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely expected to keep its key rate unchanged at a record low of 0.50% on Thursday, but signal that it is likely to rise in September. "Economic data have developed broadly in line with expectations, suggesting that factors motivating a rate hike in September remain intact," says Erica Blomgren, a strategist at \uc2 SEB\uc2 . And with a new Monetary Policy Report due only in September, the \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  is likely to stick to its June macroeconomic projections. Thursday's policy announcement, due at 0800 GMT, is hence expected to be "a non-event for markets," Ms Blomgren says. (nina.adam@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0854 GMT - \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  gilt yields fall slightly after U.K.'s inflation data released by the \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  on Wednesday showed a 2.5% year-on-year increase in July, largely in line with expectations. The yield on the U.K. 10-year government bond yields falls to 1.257% after the release, from 1.261% beforehand, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0851 GMT - Inflation in the U.K. rose by 2.5% year-on-year in July, a slighter bigger increase than the 2.4% figure in June, but in line with expectations seen in a WSJ poll of economists. Sterling reacts only slightly to the CPI report, with GBP/USD falling to 1.2708, from 1.2718 beforehand. EUR/GBP remains at 0.8914, around the same levels it was before the release. \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  raised interest rates earlier this month, but highlighted risks of a no-deal Brexit, which means it would take much stronger data to trigger a rise in the pound due to expectations of higher rates. The derivatives market suggests a 60% chance of another rate increase in the next 12 months, analysts say. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0849 GMT - The impact of the Turkish lira crisis on the eurozone economy has so far been limited but if the crisis drags on or deepens, it has the potential to affect the economy and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's policy, says \uc2 Nomura\uc2 . Turkey's crisis adds an "extra dimension" for the \uc2 ECB\uc2  to take into account, \uc2 Nomura\uc2 's analysts say, and therefore they expect this to be a key discussion topic at the ECB Governing Council's September meeting. However, the \uc2 ECB\uc2  is unlikely to change either its forward guidance on interest rates or on the net asset purchases in response to Turkey's issues, \uc2 Nomura\uc2 's analysts add. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 15, 2018 06:32 ET (10:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180815ee8f00194\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
