\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-HKMA  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  as  \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong \highlight\uc2  dollar weakens, buys HK$9.5 bln in US trade\b0\par\par\uc2 346 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2018\par 04:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail throughout)\par \par \uc2 * HKMA buys a total of HK$9.5 bln in U.S. trade on Wed\par \par \uc2 * HK dollar at 7.8495 in early Thursday trade\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK/HONG KONG, May 17 (Reuters) - \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and bought another HK$4.710 billion ($600 million) in Hong Kong dollars on Wednesday U.S. time as the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit the weaker end of its trading range.\par \par \uc2 That was in addition to HK$4.789 billion in Hong Kong dollars that the city's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought earlier during New York trading hours.\par \par \uc2 Reuters data shows the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will reduce the forecast aggregate balance - the sum of balances on clearing accounts maintained by banks with the authority - to HK$117.431 billion on May 18, when the withdrawn funds will be settled.\par \par \uc2 These HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are the first since mid-April. It has bought a total of HK$11.069 billion this week.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar is pegged at 7.8 to the U.S. dollar but can trade between 7.75 and 7.85. Under the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, the HKMA is obliged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar hits 7.75 or 7.85 to keep the band intact.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded at 7.8496 against the U.S. dollar at 0230 GMT.\par \par \uc2 HKMA Chief Executive \uc2 Norman Chan\uc2  said on Tuesday that capital flowing out from the Hong Kong dollar will allow the Hong Kong dollar's interest rates to normalise eventually, like the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Last month, the HKMA said it had confidence in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the U.S. dollar and will stay vigilant to ensure the former British colony's monetary and financial stability.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 7.8496 Hong Kong dollars) (Reporting by Saqib Iqbal Ahmed in New York and Twinnie Siu in Hong Kong; Editing by James Pomfret and Eric Meijer) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-5-17T03:38:48.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180517ee5h003bm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
