\par\par \b \uc2 PREVIEW-Hong Kong\u8217\'20\'19s 2017 GDP growth momentum seen continuing in Q1\b0\par\par\uc2 481 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2018\par 07:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Economist\uc2  sees Q1 growth at 3.1 pct y/y, near Q4 pace\par \par \uc2 * Red-hot property market a persistent risk\par \par \uc2 * Q1 GDP data due at 0830 GMT on Friday, May 11\par \par \uc2 By James Pomfret\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, May 10 (Reuters) - Hong Kong's economy likely maintained steady growth in the first quarter, despite the looming risk of a trade war between China and the United States, thanks to still-robust domestic demand.\par \par \uc2 Growth in Hong Kong is highly reliant on capital, trade, tourist and investment flows from China. A surge in domestic spending, a rise in mainland visitors and improved retail spending helped ramp up gross domestic product last year after a difficult 2016.\par \par \uc2 In spite of recent financial market volatility including declines in the Hang Seng Index, and the Hong Kong dollar weakening to 33-year lows, some analysts see last year's momentum as continuing in January-March.\par \par \uc2 "We expect domestic demand to stay as the key driver of growth, and the Hong Kong economy as a whole to continue to benefit from the improving global economy," said Thomas Shik, \uc2 Hang Seng Bank\uc2 's head of economic research.\par \par \uc2 Shik expects that GDP expanded 3.1 percent in the first quarter from a year earlier, compared with 3.4 percent in October-December. For all of 2018, the economist forecasts 3.3 percent growth.\par \par \uc2 The former British colony grew 3.8 percent in 2017 - the fastest pace in six years - helped by a robust real estate sector and stronger tourist numbers. The government forecasts 3-4 percent GDP growth in 2018, while underlying inflation of 2.5 percent is expected.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar, which is pegged to the U.S. dollar and allowed to fluctuate within a narrow trading band of 7.75 to 7.85 per U.S. dollar, in recent months has near the upper limit.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has said the weakening is partly due to expectations of faster rate hikes by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  widening interbank spreads.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's astronomical property valuations, among the world's highest, are also seen by analysts as a tangible risk as U.S. interest rates trend higher.\par \par \uc2 The city's interbank lending rates have climbed to the highest since 2008 since the HKMA started \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which could push up the cost of mortgages and raise the risk of a correction in property prices, good news for homebuyers but not for banks or the outlook for consumer spending\par \par \uc2 In spite of official efforts to cool prices, analysts see a 5 to 20 percent rise in them this year due to liquidity and a severe supply-demand imbalance. (Reporting by James Pomfret, Twinnie Siu and Donny Kwok; Editing by Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-5-10T06:15:08.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180510ee5a007px\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
