\par\par \b \uc2 More \uc2 HKMA \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Likely Soon -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,984 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2018\par 04:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0245 GMT - As the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar has pushed back to the brink of requiring additional government support, a near-term cooldown in local IPO activity--which temporarily boosted demand for HK dollars--will provide fresh pressure to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and require additional \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to again defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, says \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  . The bank also notes May has historically been a month of poor performance for both the local equities market and the Hong Kong dollar. That said, StanChart says June and July could mark the beginning of a turning point for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on fund inflows and IPO activity, which may drive the dollar down from the HK$7.85 barrier. It's currently around HK$7.8495. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT - The \uc2 RBA\uc2  has welcomed a slowdown in what was surging home-price growth and rapid household-debt accumulation. But the Aussie \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also knows it must be careful not to trigger a collapse in confidence and hefty price drops. It is watching credit conditions and warns that added scrutiny on banks as a result of a government inquiry into their activities could see mortgage lending restrained even further. Some economists are warning that could sap consumer confidence and produce a slowdown in GDP growth. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0153 GMT - \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains upbeat on the country's economic growth, predicting 3%+ expansion rates through 2019. That's good news for Treasurer Scott Morrision, who can look forward to strong tax revenues ahead of an election expected early next year. Add in slightly better inflation forecasts and rising employment and the picture improves further. Morrison will announce the government's FY budget proposal Tuesday. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT - A jump in the Nikkei Singapore PMI to 55.6 in from 53.7 in March contrasts with the official reading which showed a slowdown. The Nikkei PMI, which primarily measures private-sector activity, showed faster expansion in output and new orders while saying that private-sector capacity pressures are mounting with backlogs rising. Also, inflationary pressures are intensifying and wages are rising sharply, says the report compiled by \uc2 IHS Markit\uc2  . (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT - \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has raised its near-term core-inflation forecast a quarter-point to 2% while its mid-2018 unemployment prediction rises the same to 5.5%. Both were widely expected. Meanwhile, the \uc2 RBA\uc2  said in its quarterly report that if the economy remains improving gradually, higher interest rates will eventually be required. Still, there is no pressing case to do so as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  changed nothing about its presumption that inflation will rise at a snail's pace in coming years. That might imply that the \uc2 RBA\uc2  will be sidelined for longer than currently expected. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT - China fixed the yuan 0.3% stronger against the dollar, the most since late March and after a string of weaker fixes. The \uc2 PBoC\uc2  set the dollar's reference rate at CNY6.3521, compared with CNY6.3732 yesterday, after the dollar broadly pulled back Thursday. The WSJ Dollar Index saw its biggest drop in 6 weeks. The dollar ended at CNY6.3532 in onshore trading Thursday. (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 1738 GMT - Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held an unscheduled meeting today to hike its policy rate by 300bps to 33.25% in an attempt to stem the rapid depreciation of the peso. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it took the action to "guarantee the process of disinflation and is ready to act again if necessary." This is the second time in a week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hiked the rate unexpectedly, which hasn't stopped the peso's slide. \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen 6% against the dollar since the first rate hike last Friday, and that the "crisis looks set to continue unless the government steps in to reassure investors that it will take more aggressive steps to fix Argentina's economic vulnerabilities." (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1548 GMT - Having reached on Wednesday a 12-week high of 110.03, USD/JPY has the potential to rise above key chart resistance around 110.22, the 200-day moving average, say \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  technical analysts. But USD/JPY is last down 0.8% at 108.98, and "110.02 is currently holding the topside," the analysts say. If this remains the case, it "would allow for a dip lower" in USD/JPY, possibly down to 108.58, where it is likely to find support, they say. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1457 GMT - GBP/USD is trading close to is 200-day moving average at 1.3537, according to Factset, and this is likely to cause some consolidation, say \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  technical analysts. GBP/USD is last down 0.3% at 1.3544. "Intraday rallies are indicated to terminate at circa 1.3715/1.3815 and we have divergence on the intraday charts," \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analysts say, adding that they would forecast "some near term profit taking." (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1447 GMT - EUR/USD volatility is expected to rise in the medium term, says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  . Eurozone rates should catch up with U.S. rates, tightening interest rate differentials, "given inevitable \uc2 ECB\uc2  policy normalization and the late-cycle dynamics of USD rates," SocGen says. Therefore, it recommends buying a EUR/USD forward volatility agreement of six months in six months, which means getting an option to buy a six-month EUR/USD forward volatility in six months. EUR/USD is last up by 0.1% at 1.1962. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1437 GMT - In its rate announcement Wednesday, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  said "inflation on a 12-month basis is expected to run near the Committee's symmetric 2% objective over the medium term." \uc2 Saxo Bank\uc2  says the introduction of the word "symmetric" is an "indication that the Fed isn't about to lose its cool if inflation rises further in coming months now that the 2% target has effectively been reached." This goes against with what some strategists have been saying: that the Fed could accelerate its monetary policy tightening on the back of stronger economic data and good inflation growth. The dollar last trades flat against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, with the DXY index at 92.5410. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 Inflation in the eurozone fell unexpectedly to 1.2% April from 1.3% in March, although partly as a result of the timing of Easter. Core inflation tumbled to 0.7% in April from 1% in March, hitting the lowest level since March 2017. The data follows weak first-quarter GDP data on Wednesday, leaving it more likely that the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will wait until July to make a decision on exiting its quantitative easing program. The following is a selection of analyst comments. 1111 GMT - The flash estimate of April eurozone inflation continued to show muted price dynamics that won't push the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to "rush for the QE exit with undue haste at their June meeting," says \uc2 Oliver Rakau\uc2 , chief German economist at Oxford Economics. While headline inflation fell to 1.2% in April, from 1.3% in March, "the real news was that core inflation tumbled to 0.7% after having averaged 1.0% in the previous three months," marking the lowest increase since April 2015. Energy and food inflation increased, largely compensating the weakness of underlying price pressures, Rakau says. Neither markets nor the \uc2 ECB\uc2  will be shocked by this data but it came amid a growth outlok clouded by increasing uncertainty, Rakau says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) 1125 GMT - The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will look through volatility in eurozone inflation data, says \uc2 Florian Hense\uc2 , economist at Berenberg. The decline in April inflation--which was greater than expected in headline, core and services inflation measures--is largely due to the timing of Easter and should correct itself the next month, he says. Beyond a temporary oil effect this year, which may push inflation up to 1.7% or even 1.8% in the summer months, the medium-term outlook remains benign. Underlying inflation looks set to firm very gradually, allowing the \uc2 ECB\uc2  to stick to its schedule of reducing monetary stimulus cautiously, he says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) 1129 GMT - Danske expects the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to see through the temporary inflation dip of April and focus on the trend in the underlying inflation pressures and whether core inflation rebounds above 1.0% in May. "This will also be crucial for whether the next change in the forward guidance will come in June (as markets expect) or July (as we expect)," says \uc2 Danske\uc2  analyst Aila Mihr. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) April's eurozone inflation data "probably represent downside news to the \uc2 ECB\uc2  ," says \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Chris Hare. He says the April data might offer a "cleaner read" of the underlying picture than they did March, which was subject to temporary upward pressure from the timing of Easter and possibly also the wintery weather. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  sees two reasons for the "very soft--perhaps even a softening--underlying inflation environment." These include further evidence that the lagging impact of the euro's rises last year has kicked in and of the fact that domestically generated inflation remains very subdued, Mr. Hare says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) 1152 GMT - Inflation growth in eurozone was smaller in April than in March, falling to 1.2% from 1.3%, and below market expectations of 1.4%, according to a WSJ poll. But "following a very strong recovery last year...it was inevitable that growth in the eurozone would slow to more sustainable levels," says \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  . EUR/USD is last up 0.2% at 1.1971. However, the reasons which previously pushed investors to buy euros aren't there anymore. "The eurozone is no longer producing the upside surprises that were characteristic of much of last year," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. Given speculators are still quite long of euros, the euro is "vulnerable to this news flow." (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga) 1251 GMT - Weaker inflation in services in the eurozone in April "will reflect the effects of this year's earlier Easter," says \uc2 Cathal Kennedy\uc2  at \uc2 RBC\uc2 . The Easter-induced\par \par \uc2 3 May 2018 22:45 ET  More \uc2 HKMA\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Likely Soon -- Market Talk -2-\par \par \uc2 decline in services will also be the primary reason behind the slowdown in core inflation in April, the European economist says. As for the takeaway for the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  , neither the fall in the core nor the headline reading will come as much of a surprise, as both developments were well sign-posted, "though the extent of the drop in core inflation may cause some concern." (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) 1347 GMT - The eurozone's April inflation data suggest that moderation centered on components linked to holiday timings, such as air fares and recreational items, which points to an imminent rebound but will still cause concern for the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  , say \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  analysts. \uc2 Luigi Speranza\uc2 , head of European economics, and Gizem Kara, senior eurozone economist, think the peak in activity is past, but say that economic growth--though slowing--should remain solid and above the trend rate. Along with a sudden slowdown in growth in 1Q, "these data will still be a source of concern for the \uc2 ECB\uc2  , suggesting caution," they say. That said, they note that leading short-term indicators of price pressures are edging higher. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 03, 2018 22:45 ET (02:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180504ee5400082\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
