\par\par \b \uc2 Mortgage-Rate Hikes May Follow Hibor's Gains -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,225 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2018\par 08:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0656 GMT - The \uc2 HKMA\uc2 's market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  within the past week to bolster the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar has been pushing up depressed interbank lending rates. With Hibor's recent gains, DBS says it's possible that local lenders will raise their benchmark mortgage rate, or prime rate, in 3 quarter-point increments to 5.75% by year's end. But the investment bank adds that it won't impact Hong Kong's stock market. Its range for the \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  remains at 29500-33500. The index is up 0.7% at 30278. (john.wu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT - The FTSE 100 will likely open 19 points higher at 7245, according to \uc2 London Capital Group\uc2  , tracking a rise in Asian and U.S. stocks as upbeat earnings boost sentiment. Concerns about tensions between the U.S. and Russia, the possibility of military action in Syria and a potential trade war between the U.S. and China are fading for now and "U.S. earning season has filled the void," says \uc2 Jasper Lawler\uc2 , analyst at LCG. More gains in the pound could weigh on U.K. stocks, and investors will be watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reaction to U.K. inflation data at 0830 GMT. Property stocks will likely be in focus after Hammerson said an acquisition of Intu was no longer in the best interest of shareholders. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0640 GMT - German Bunds remain stuck in their "miniscule" trading ranges with the 10-year Bund yields just above 0.50%, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  rates strategist Christoph Rieger. Early Wednesday the 10-year Bund yield is trading at 0.51%, up 0.3 basis points, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . Against this backdrop, Germany's upcoming EUR3 billion tap of the February 2028 Bund is "unlikely to cause a larger splash," Rieger says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0628 GMT - The Nikkei closed above 22000 for the 1st time in nearly 7 weeks as geopolitical tensions eased and investors positioned themselves for earnings season. That as equities both locally and in the US are benefiting from "a short squeeze" amid share buybacks, says Sean Darby, chief global equity strategist with Jefferies. The BoJ's ETF buying is also helping, he adds. Meanwhile, today's risk-on action in Asia sent the yen lower, further helping Japanese stocks. The Nikkei rose 1.4% to 22158.20 as the dollar is at Y107.32, versus Y107.02 when local stock trading opened. (suryatapa.bhattacharya@wsj.com; @SuryatapaB)\par \par \uc2 0552 GMT - While a slowing economy may be part of the \uc2 PBoC\uc2 's thinking yesterday's cut in the reserve-requirement ratio, Smartkarma's Christopher Balding posits that more-fundamental concerns such as banks' constrained capacity to lend and the potential shifting of shadow-banking assets could have been the primary impetus to act. Those concerns are also highlighted amid the recent wave of banks' capital raising in both the onshore and offshore markets, he adds. Shares of Hong Kong-listed mainland lenders are outperforming the broader market there with China Construction and \uc2 ICBC\uc2  both up more than 1%. (john.wu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0529 GMT - Record numbers of visitors from China, the US and India came Down Under in the year through February. Tourism makes up about 10% of Australia's total exports, and a new report from Tourism and Transport Forum Australia predicts tourism levels rising 31% from 2016-26 to 385 million annual visits. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0503 GMT - \uc2 Logan Property\uc2  is marketing a 3-year dollar bond at around 7.125% after having raised S$200 million ($152 million) last week after selling similar debt at 6.125%. It's one of many high-yield Chinese property companies that are looking to raise fresh funds before funding costs go up further. Still, each successive sale is carrying higher coupons. For instance, CIFI raised $500 million from 3-year bonds at a yield of 6.875% last week; it paid 5.625% in January. The company is already sounding investors for a new deal, a source familiar with the matter says. Meanwhile, Singapore's First REIT is also looking to borrow. (manju.dalal@wsj.com; @manjudalalsg)\par \par \uc2 0416 GMT - Chinese tech company \uc2 Huawei\uc2  will start meeting global fixed-income investors today regarding a potential 10-year dollar-bond offering what would be its initial euro-denominated issue. It comes as the US is clamping down on Chinese tech companies, citing security concerns, with \uc2 ZTE\uc2  already on the line. How much investors will demand from \uc2 Huawei\uc2  remains to be seen, but other tech heavyweights such as \uc2 Baidu\uc2  had to pay higher pricing of late. Proven Glory Capital will be the issuer of \uc2 Huawei\uc2  bonds, with the euro deal like in the 5/7-year range. The firm may even consider a long-dated dollar tranche depending upon investor appetite and market conditions. (manju.dalal@wsj.com; @manjudalalsg)\par \par \uc2 0402 GMT - The \uc2 PBoC\uc2  is shifting into credit-saving mode, contends Daiwa after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserve-ratio cut. The investment bank says credit conditions are tight now in China, and that the \uc2 PBoC\uc2  has to make a tradeoff between credit and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Daiwa, unlike others, considers the ratio reduction a shift to monetary-easing mode and "a hedge against a potential trade war with the US." Daiwa expects further reductions and pressure on the yuan. The firm reiterates its call for the dollar rising to CNY7 at year's end. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0341 GMT - Despite a solid 1Q GDP reading, China's economic will slow, says \uc2 UBS\uc2  . The investment bank thinks more-visible signs of softening will emerge around midyear and it expects an upcoming deceleration of property activity and further infrastructure-investment slowdown to be key growth headwinds for the rest of 2018. Then there's potential trade friction. Meanwhile, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says some muted readings for March were likely the result of pollution-related production cuts and the later-than-usual restarting of local public-works activity. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0328 GMT - Exports are important in any Asian economic cycle, it is more so at the present time, says ANZ. Asia entered this cycle with poor corporate profitability, high debt, and high labor costs. Good progress in this direction has been made until now. But, March's manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2  data has turned out to be weak for all economies. Whether this marks the beginning of a downturn in the region's business cycle, or global and Asian growth have entered a late stage of the economic cycle, or the March readings are a one-off aberration is unclear at this stage, ANZ says. (james.glynn@wsj.com;@JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0246 GMT - Hong Kong's Grade A office market remained active in 1Q, says \uc2 Cushman & Wakefield\uc2  , with rents rising in all submarkets on the back of solid demand. The pace of decentralization, especially from tenants in the professional-services sector, accelerated as prime Greater Central rentals hit a record HK$134/square foot ($17) monthly. The real-estate-services firm says tenants are "increasingly evaluating the viability of decentralized areas." (john.wu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 18, 2018 02:56 ET (06:56 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180418ee4i000jb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
