\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 FX Interventions \highlight\uc2  Increase in Size -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,129 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2018\par 04:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0254 GMT - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar this morning continues to hover at the weak end of its permitted trading range against the greenback after hitting it last week. The \uc2 HKMA\uc2  was forced sell US dollars twice on Friday during New York trading-- US$397 million and US$429 million in separate \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar. That means the magnitude of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has continued to increase since first taking action Thursday. The \uc2 HKMA\uc2  has sold a total of US$1.23 billion, resulting in purchases of HK$9.66 billion, so far this month to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg. (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 0245 GMT - Asia's dollar-bond market has opened with a bang today, poised to see more than $10 billion of deals that almost totals all of last week's action. Chinese companies are rushing to refinance; \uc2 Central China Real Estate\uc2  , Sunac, Yanlord, Jingrui and Hanwha Life are among those marketing bonds. Meanwhile, Singapore's \uc2 United Overseas Bank\uc2  is out with a 3-year deal. Chinese property firms Guangzhou and Yuexiu REIT have announced bond mandates, along with China Hongqiao, China Overseas Finance, \uc2 Philippine National Bank\uc2  and Indonesia's \uc2 Alam Sutera Realty\uc2  . (manju.dalal@wsj.com; @manjudalalsg)\par \par \uc2 0237 GMT - New Zealand's 1Q CPI data this week is likely to be underwhelming. The government announced free first-year college education, which will pull inflation lower. But higher tobacco taxes could somewhat offset. There is still little evidence of a sustained pickup in inflation. Tradables prices are no longer consistently falling, but there has been no real lift in non-tradables inflation in recent years either. \uc2 Westpac\uc2  expects CPI of +0.5% on quarter and +1.1% on-year, the lowest since September 2016. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0233 GMT - \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  setting a 2-3% inflation target 25 years ago was largely on faith, notes deputy \uc2 RBA\uc2  Gov. \uc2 Guy Debelle\uc2  . "There was very little in the way of academic analysis to provide guidance about a general design and \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  principles." But now, the "proof of the pudding has been in the eating," he says, with the framework helping to deliver sustained GDP growth. Still, Debelle believes it remains important to question the target and whether enhancements are needed. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT - Economists are anticipating tomorrow's 1Q GDP report to show a slight downtick. The median forecast of 11 economists surveyed by WSJ is for economic growth to have been 6.7%, versus 4Q's 6.8%. Also out then will be March industrial production, with its growth seen slowing to 6.3% from the combined 7.2% figure for January and February. Economic growth will likely face more headwinds in 2H as deleveraging is gaining traction, say Macquarie economists. Meanwhile, March fixed-asset investment growth out Tuesday is projected to have eased to 7.7% for 1Q versus 7.9% in January and February. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0125 GMT - China guides the yuan slightly stronger against the dollar, setting today's trading midpoint at CNY6.2884, versus CNY6.2898 on Friday. The dollar ended onshore trading then at CNY6.2825. Late Friday, Treasury stayed away from naming China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator. The onshore yuan has appreciated 3.6% this year, a bit more than \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in general have so far in 2018. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT - South Korea remains on Treasury's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -manipulation watch list despite the won's 13% jump versus the dollar last year, thanks to the country's trade and current-account surplus. But the agency also flagged \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns. "The won is not notably strong compared to levels it has been over the last couple decades." Meanwhile, "there was a notable and concerning pickup in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in November and January "that appears to have been for the purpose of slowing won appreciation against the dollar." The greenback this year has been around levels last seen in late 2014 versus the won. "Treasury will continue to monitor closely Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices and urges the authorities to report its exchange-rate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a transparent and timely manner." The pair is steady this morning, like most in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 0019 GMT - Some pep might be returning to Australia's property market with successful residential sales at auction rising in all but 2 capital cities in 1Q versus 4Q, says \uc2 CoreLogic\uc2  . Sydney, the country's biggest market, logged the biggest increase with 64% of auctions being successful from 4Q's 58%. Melbourne logged the highest rate at 68.4%, versus 68.1% in 4Q. But nationally, the successful-sales rate fell 10% from a year earlier in 1Q. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0007 GMT - New Zealand firms' earnings forecasts have been falling since mid-2017, and ANZ says that's more a factor of rising costs than cooled revenue hopes. The investment bank isn't worried about the margin pressure and advocates price hikes to offset the expense increases. That, of course, would push up inflation rates--something \uc2 New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't oppose. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2350 GMT - As many agree that Thursday's 1Q CPI report from New Zealand won't mean much policy- or other-wise, ANZ says the trend for prices is still higher. A cyclical low in wage inflation has occurred due to skill shortages, meaning pay hikes will pick up, the bank contends. But the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be in any hurry to act. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2334 GMT - New Zealand's services sector strengthened last month, says \uc2 BNZ\uc2 , with its metric hitting a 14-month high of 58.8. February's reading was 55.3. Despite breaking out of a tight band seen since September, \uc2 BNZ\uc2  says caution is needed as Easter timing and weather could have impacted the results. The report may overstate underlying trends, the bank adds. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2324 GMT [Dow Jones ] New Zealand vegetable prices rose 9.5% in March led by tomatoes, lettuce, cauliflower, cabbage, and broccoli prices, Stats NZ says. Vegetable crops have been affected by a run of storms in recent weeks; lower supply due to bad weather usually means higher prices. Tomatoes rose more than 60%, while lettuce prices were up 20% in the same period, and cauliflower rose 85%. Cabbage prices rose 50%. Overall, food prices rose 1% in March. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 15, 2018 22:54 ET (02:54 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180416ee4g00018\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
