\par\par \b \uc2 Hong Kong Dollar May Not Move for a While -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,280 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2018\par 08:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0609 GMT - The Hong Kong dollar touching the weak end of its trading band with the greenback is "eventful but insignificant," contends ANZ. It also calls the HKD purchases the past day by the \uc2 HKMA\uc2  (the first since the current band was put in place 13 years ago) to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling further a passive method to withdraw liquidity. "This action is eventful, but it poses little risk on the financial market." The investment bank adds that "without strong speculative flows from global investors, we believe the peg will function as usual." ANZ also predicts that the Hibor-Libor spread, which has been key to the Hong Kong dollar's weakness, is unlikely to narrow sharply amid ample banking-system liquidity conditions. So HK$7.85 could be the exchange rate for a while. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0557 GMT - China's recently hot trade activity is seen slowing the rest of this year, says Macquarie. A rising yuan and slowing PPI should cap exports, the investment bank posits, while import growth should slow as "the Chinese economy should be at a late-cycle stage." But it does predict Beijing will look to boost US imports to cut into China's politically fraught trade surplus with America--which hit a new high in 1Q. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0503 GMT - Folks should be skeptical just how much the US--and Trump in particular--might be regarding being part of TPP, \uc2 OCBC\uc2  economist \uc2 Selena Ling\uc2  tells WSJ. She adds there's also the matter of whether the 11 countries currently partaking would want to reopen negotiations to potentially get the US back in. Trump tweeted Thursday night that the country "would only join TPP if the deal were substantially better than the deal offered to Pres. Obama ." (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0457 GMT - Following impacts from the Lunar New Year in February, Indonesia is seen having moved back to having a trade surplus last month. The median forecast of 10 economists polled by WSJ is for a $182.4 million surplus, versus February's $116 million trade deficit. "Indonesia's exports are doing well thanks to buoyant global demand. However, palm oil is a sore point, with exports slumping in February" after India's recent import-tariff increase, notes \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics. \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  has expected rising investment in Indonesia could boost imports this year and widen the country's current-account deficit to above 2% of GDP. The ratio was 1.7% in 2017. The March trade data are due Monday. (i-made.sentana@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0445 GMT - Is the \uc2 RBA\uc2  prepping markets for another GDP-growth downgrade? \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  thinks so. \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  originally talked of 3-4% GDP growth this year, then shaved that down to 3%. But both last month and this, it said, "the bank's central forecast is for the Australian economy to grow faster in 2018" than last year's 2.3%. \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says the \uc2 RBA\uc2  has therefore effectively lowered its 2018 growth forecast in words, if not in numbers. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0419 GMT - The move by US to potentially rejoin the Trans-Pacific Partnership a little over a year after withdrawing from the trade deal hints at Washington's plans to strategically refocus on the Indo-Pacific region amid trade tensions with China. Given that China is not in TPP, this may be an effort for the US to divert US-China trade to US-rest-of-Asia trade, says Jeff Ng, chief Asia economist for Continuum Economics. Still, Asia has already partially decoupled from trade with the US and moved more closely towards trade with China, he adds. "Trade frictions and rhetoric will likely remain in focus for the coming months, we don't expect things to get resolved so soon," he says. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0412 GMT - Don't make much of China's surprise March trade deficit. A "large trade surplus is almost certain to return in April," says Capital Economics ' Julian Evans-Pritchard, as seasonal factors weighing on exports fade. (lin.zhu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0412 GMT - China's trade surplus with the US continues to grow, with the imbalance inevitably continuing due to the countries' diverging economic policies, says Ding Shuang, an economist \uc2 Standard Chartered Bank\uc2 . Washington's expansionary fiscal policy contrasts with Beijing's deleveraging efforts, which means the trade gap between the 2 is going to worsen, although China's global trade is becoming more balanced, he says. "What Trump looks at is bilateral trade data, not China's total trade, so today's figure is definitely going to complicate any trade talks to ease trade tension," he says. For the first 3 months of 2018, China's trade surplus with the US reached $58.25 billion, up 19.4% on year, while its total trade surplus was $48.39 billion, down nearly 20%.(liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0352 GMT - The pickup in activity in Singapore's real estate market is expected to boost the construction sector, giving a further fillip to the country's economy, \uc2 OCBC\uc2  says. Construction has been a weak spot in the GDP growth mix in recent years as authorities clamped down to cool the property sector 4 years ago. On a quarter-on-quarter seasonally adjusted basis, construction grew by 4.1% in 1Q after contracting in last 3 quarters. \uc2 OCBC\uc2  economist \uc2 Selena Ling\uc2  says the recent frenzy of collective property sales and improved interest in private residential property have not yet filtered through to actual construction activity. "But the pipeline should start to build in the coming quarters if not years," she adds. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0339 GMT - Australia's banks face less interest-rate risk than their international peers, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  argues. For the system as a whole, liabilities due to mature in one month exceed assets that will mature in that time by over A$1 trillion. However, the \uc2 RBA\uc2  says it's repricing maturity that matters here. Unlike in many other countries, Aussie banks have more assets that can be repriced within one month than they do liabilities. That's primarily because about 80% of domestic housing loans are priced using variable interest rates that can move with short-term interest rates, and most Aussie business loans are priced at a fixed premium to the 3-month bank bill swap rate, the \uc2 RBA\uc2  says in its latest Financial Stability Review. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 0325 GMT - Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  flagged potential inflation pressures in tightening today. But \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  isn't convinced reality will cooperate. The investment bank doesn't see core inflation topping 2%, a level that could prompt further tightening, predicting a 2H average of 1.8%. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0308 GMT - As Hong Kong's de-facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent HK$3.26 billion ($415 million) within the past day to prop up the local dollar, \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  thinks the \uc2 HKMA\uc2  may reallocate its portfolio. As of the end of 2016, only 7.8% wasn't in the US or \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars. Meanwhile, the investment bank believes \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could total some HK$80 billion the next month or two. That compares with more than HK$640 billion during the financial crisis and HK$147 billion after 2015's Chinese yuan devaluation. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 13, 2018 02:09 ET (06:09 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180413ee4d000fk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
