\par\par \b \uc2 Hong Kong Not Seen Having to Prop Up Dollar -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,167 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2018\par 03:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0213 GMT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's de-facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, predicts Standard Chartered 's Eddie Cheung, as the city's dollar is just shy of the low end of its trading band versus the greenback. The \uc2 HKMA\uc2  would step in at HK$7.85, and the dollar is currently around HK$7.8465. He sees better prospects for emerging markets in the wake of a perceived less hawkish Fed, which may bring in money to Asia and Hong Kong--boosting the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and reducing the allure of the carry trade. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT - On his third day as \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  deputy governor, \uc2 Masazumi Wakatabe\uc2  has been summoned to parliament to take questions from a lawmaker shortly before 0815 GMT Thursday. At his inaugural press conference Tuesday, Wakatabe said he would propose additional easing "without hesitation" if it's judged necessary. While he said the best prescription to beat deflation is to expand the quantity of money, he refused to give any further details of possible easing measures. (megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT - A draft of a massive spending bill Congressional leaders released tonight includes a provision that would scale back changes to a tip-pooling regulation proposed last year by the Labor Department and backed by the restaurant industry. The bill, if passed by Congress, would prohibit an employer from keeping the tips given by patrons to workers. The bill specifically bars tip sharing with managers and supervisors. In a regulation change proposed in December, the Trump administration sought to allow employers the option to mandate restaurant servers and other workers who receive tips share that money with nontipped colleagues, undoing an Obama-era prohibition on the practice. Worker advocates say doing so would open the door for restaurant owners to skim tips. (eric.morath@wsj.com; @ericmorath)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT - The door is most definitely not closed to 4 Fed rate hikes this year, says \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  portfolio manager Nick Peters. "In essence, the FOMC is now perfectly split between three and four hikes, and all but gone is the tail of those who thought there were downside risks to that." Wednesday's releases and comments merely mean that "a more-forceful turn towards hawkishness--which had been hinted in a couple of recent Fed speeches by Powell and others--did not materialize," added Peters. He thinks the new Fed chief "wants us to know that the Fed are still in no rush to get ahead of the curve." (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT - China boosted the yuan by the most against the dollar in nearly a month Thursday after the greenback fell overnight. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened the yuan by 0.4% against the dollar, the most since Feb. 27, as it fixed the dollar's reference rate at CNY6.3167 versus CNY6.3396 on Wednesday. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raised rates on Wednesday, but the WSJ Dollar Index notched its biggest one-day decline since Jan. 24. The dollar ended at CNY6.3315 on Wednesday. (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT - \uc2 Noble Group\uc2  goes on a trading halt in Singapore soon after confirming its first bond default. The surprise trading halt is pending an announcement, Noble says. With the default on US$379 million of fixed rate senior notes already factored in--shares have declined 24% this week--the announcement is likely to be keenly watched for any progress on its proposed debt restructuring deal that has been opposed by the company's equity investors. Shares are down nearly 50% since the start of the year to a 19-year low. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0111 GMT - The 17,500 rise in Australian employment in February continued the record run of monthly job gains, but the rise in the unemployment rate from 5.5% to 5.6%, and the increase in underemployment highlights that wage growth is unlikely to rise soon, says \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  . Even after well over 400,000 jobs have been created since early 2017, spare capacity in the labor market has grown. (james.glynn@wsj.com ; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0053 GMT - The current pace of job creation in Australia implies it will take at least a year of continued growth before the economy uses up all the spare capacity in the labor market, says BIS Oxford Economics. A significant number of the jobs being created have been filled by newcomers to the workforce, particularly women and older workers. As a result the unemployment rate is not falling. In fact, it rose last month to 5.6%. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0051 GMT - Australia's unemployment rate rose to 5.6% last month and other measures of job market slack nudged up, overall meaning a not-good reading for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It's counting on a tightening employment sector to eventually spark higher wages, in the process fueling a bit more inflation. It could be that the job market is showing some signs of a hangover after stellar job creation in 2017. Still, there were 17,500 net new jobs for February, so the impressive run of uninterrupted hiring goes on. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT - The dollar's slide versus the yen accelerated in recent minutes after triggering stop-loss orders below Wednesday's bottom of Y105.88, says \uc2 Aozora Bank\uc2  's Akira Moroga. Selling of dollars by Japanese exporters is also likely taking place ahead of the Japanese FY-end in 9 days, he adds. The dollar is now at Y105.72, versus Y106 at the top of the hour. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2339 GMT - Dollar bears have the green light after the FOMC failed to embrace a more-hawkish tack, says Oanda's Stephen Innes. The greenback could be "extremely vulnerable" amid normalization rhetoric possibly coming out of the \uc2 ECB\uc2  and expectations from some for further yen gains, he adds. The WSJ Dollar Index slid 0.7% on Wednesday, its biggest drop in 2 months, and it's off a further 0.2% in Asia this morning. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0005 GMT - Should the Australian dollar really be higher? Of course not, says Axi trader, describing the latest gain as merely a reflection of market disappointment that the Fed's dot plot stuck with 2 more rate increases this year instead of 3. Fresh from hitting a low of 0.7671 Wednesday, the Aussie hit a high of 0.7779 early in Asia for a gain of more than 1 US cent after it climbed along with the euro following the Fed's latest rate increase and dot plot. Ultimately though, the Fed was as hawkish as traders expected for rates after this year, it adds. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 21, 2018 22:13 ET (02:13 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180322ee3m0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
